TY  - CHAP
TI  - Player-Character Is What You Are in the Dark. The Phenomenology of Immersion in Dungeons & Dragons
AU  - White, William J.
T2  - Dungeons & Dragons and Philosophy
A2  - Robichaud, Christopher
AB  - The idea of role-playing makes some people nervous – even some people who play role-playing games (RPGs). So the idea of immersion is central to understanding how Dungeons Dragons and other aspects of participatory culture work. Phenomenology is a kind of “philosophy of mind” associated with the works of twentieth-century philosophers Edmund Husserl, Martin Heidegger, Jean-Paul Sartre, and Maurice Merleau-Ponty, among others. The domain of phenomenology encompasses the entire range of experiences in the world, paying attention to what Husserl called “intentionality,” or how our consciousness is directed toward particular objects in the world. The fact that this is presented as an atypical approach to play, or at least one that requires some effort, invites us to ask about typical modes of play if visualization of in-game events is not that mode. This chapter concluded by scratch the surface of a complete phenomenological description of role-playing.
