TY  - CHAP
TI  - Is Anyone Actually Chaotic Evil? A Playable Theory of Willful Wrongdoing
AU  - Mussett, Neil
T2  - Dungeons & Dragons and Philosophy
A2  - Robichaud, Christopher
AB  - As it turns out, accounting for the mechanics of willful wrongdoing has been a major problem for ethics from the beginning, and it has led to some very strange theories. Socrates and Plato simply deny the possibility. What does this mean for DungeonsDragons (DD)? First of all, it means that nobody chooses evil for the sake of evil, what some people call diabolic evil. The primary sources of evil are indifference and self-deception. Both lead me to a life of convention, simply living up to the code of conduct given by society. It is much more comfortable to think of evil as something done by villains in movies. There is something rather disturbing about the thought that one can become evil gradually. Because of the influence of bad habits, much about the moral life feels grey and fuzzy.
