TY  - JOUR
TI  - The Use of Structured Goal Setting in Simulation Design
AU  - Balzac, Stephen R.
T2  - Journal of Interactive Drama
AB  - Games are written for different purposes: LARPs to entertain, Serious Games to inform and educate, and simulations as training tools or to discover how people might respond to different situations. No matter the reason, they all need to keep the participants actively involved and interested in the scenario. A failure to maintain interest means that the simulation or game fails in its purpose. Structured goal setting, as described in Locke & Latham (2002), is proposed as a technique for maintaining player focus and interest, and for putting the plot into the hands of the players. Relevant research is reviewed, and application within a variety of games is discussed. Suggested methods of applying goal setting to simulation design are provided.
