AB  - Bad News is an award winning game, simulation, and performance art piece which depends on story sifting and live acting performed simultaneously and in real time by two human performers taking on two roles: a Wizard and an Actor. This paper offers a quantified analysis of eighty-one playthroughs of Bad News, identifying common patterns that were frequently used by the story sifting Wizard to unearth narratively salient content buried in the simulation to reveal to the player. This analysis informs the design of an in-development WizActor interface; a tool intended to reduce the cognitive overhead of story sifting such that future pieces of computationally assisted performance might only depend on one person to fulfill both roles.
