AB  - The theory of interactive fiction is mainly based on the Borgesian metaphor of the “garden of forking paths.” Interactive works are supposedly made of “twisty little passages” and built on several types of labyrinthine patterns: networks, trees, mazes, flowcharts, etc. This metaphor often narrows the scope of interactive fiction to works built on this model: “choose your own adventure” books; text-based computer games; hypertext narratives; experimental short movies; etc.
