TY  - JOUR
TI  - Role to Play: Examining the Player Experiences of Dungeons & Dragons
AU  - Paulino, Marinela Y.
AU  - Cuason, Sherwin U.
T2  - The Bedan Journal of Psychology
AB  - This qualitative study aimed to describe player experiences from playing Dungeons & Dragons. Research questions sought to identify which experiences were meaningful, beneficial, and appealing about the game. Through purposive non-random sampling, a total of 60 respondents answered the survey between June and August 2016. Majority of respondents were male, within the age range of 25 - 34 years, and having at least 1 - 3 years of experience. Thematic analysis was used to categorize the data acquired through an online survey. The researcher categorized keywords into three core elements: Creative, Emotional, and Social Experiences. The findings show that there is no doubt role-playing games such as D&D have the potential to entertain, heal, and educate. Psychological implications include D&D and TRPGs alike to be used in both therapeutic and educational settings. When designed thoughtfully and facilitated carefully, RPGs can help clients understand themselves on a deeper level, or discover something new about them. Even for regular players, RPGs can be a playground to exercise social and cognitive skills.
