TY  - THES
TI  - Lock-Downs and Walk-Outs: Managing and Losing Trust Within the Regulated Wispy Community of Fantasy Role-Playing
AU  - MacLean, Kristen
AB  - This participant observation study explored the underlying structural component of the tabletop fantasy game Dungeons & Dragons and examined how social order is maintained and functions in the face of social ambiguity and randomness. This study was conducted on a sample of 35 fantasy gamers within the North Bay, Ontario community. Field notes on 20 gameplay sessions were taken as well as transcriptions of conversational interviews and follow-up interviews with participants. Finding suggest that Dungeons & Dragons operates as a wispy community (Fine & Van den Scott, 2011) that demands social stratification and regulation due to the chance and ambiguity embedded within the game. In order to regulate shared fantasy, Game Masters had to engage in complex role negotiations and mechanisms of social repair. Findings indicate that for Dungeons & Dragons to operate to shared normative expectations, underlying trust and the preservation of the shared narrative must be socially upheld. A failure to maintain narrative-focused or for a Game Master to effectively navigate their social role causes players to engage in role-distancing behaviours or dismiss themselves from the wispy community completely. This research serves to highlight the importance of share social meaning and structure in all societies regardless of size (macro or micro) or stratification (rigid or wispy).
