TY  - JOUR
TI  - Dungeons & Dragons and Damsels in Distress: A Visual Rhetorical Critique of The Dungeon Master's Guide
AU  - Brennan, Mylie
T2  - Women and Language
AB  - In this essay I examine the cover art of the 1979 version of The Dungeon Master's Guide, one of the original pieces of D&D artwork. Artwork in the D&D community has shaped and continues to shape how players understand aspects of the game, primarily because the game is one of pure imagination. This particular piece, for example, depicts an efreet, which is described as a giant djinn from the fire plane. Having a visual image allows players to better understand what the creators of D&D intend the monsters to actually be like. Examining the visual rhetoric of this image reveals the ideology held and perpetuated by the original writers of the game and presumably adopted by original players. The Dungeon Master's Guide is one of the core rule books of D&D, meaning that the book is required to play the game. The pervasiveness of this image in the D&D community and its position in D&D's history makes it an ideal visual text for rhetorical criticism. In the first section of this critique, I review literature about visual rhetoric and gendered images, and I introduce Sonja K. Foss's (1994) schema for evaluating visual imagery. In the second section, I describe the visual elements of the text. In the third section, using Foss's schema, I argue that the cover art of The Dungeon Master's Guide first functions to spark the imagination of its viewers and, second, functions to invite viewers to play D&D. These functions are only partially supported due to the exaggerated depiction of gender and race in the image, meaning that the image is centered on the hegemonic ideals of heterosexual, white males and is not invitational toward women or racial minorities. Finally, I conclude by articulating the implications of my argument.
