TY  - JOUR
TI  - Cognitive Permeation between the real and the imagined: the influence of personal, fictive identities on one's own identity.
AU  - Kapitány, Rohan
AU  - Francis, Kathryn
AB  - The study of imaginative play has historically been limited to children, however recent research suggests that adults engage in pretend play far more than previously considered. Though the manner in which adults engage in such play is notably different from children, and has been termed “Pretensive Shared Reality” (PSR). A central question regarding PSR is that of the degree to which the ‘cognitive quarantine’ (Nichols and Stich, 2000) is permeable. We examine the degree of permeability between real and fictive identities, and establish a novel ‘cognitive porousness’ scale. We also established the degree to which theoretically relevant factors, such as enjoyment and engagement, influence porousness. We report that there is a notable degree of cognitive porousness between players' personal identities and their characters’ identity when players responded, via their character, to fantasy-theme moral dilemmas. Furthermore, we found that engagement influenced the degree of permeation while enjoyment appeared to have no impact. This research demonstrates the tractability of examining adult pretence from a quantitative and cognitive perspective.
