TY  - THES
TI  - Adventuring into the Imaginal: The Therapeutic Function of Mythopoesis in Tabletop Role-Playing Games
AU  - Watson, Dylan
AB  - Fantasy-based tabletop role-playing games (TTRPGs) have garnered significant attention in recent years. A substantial body of literature has been produced regarding the application of TTRPGs within the context of therapy, as an activity promoting psychological development and improved mental health. This hermeneutic study examines TTRPGs through a depth psychological framework and explores a gap in the literature, inquiring specifically about what archetypal and imaginal psychology can reveal about their psychotherapeutic value. The analysis that follows deconstructs previous suggestions made in the literature that TTRPGs benefit individuals via interpretation and subsequent interventions. The findings of this study indicate that TTRPGs serve a therapeutic function by ritualizing participation with fantasy-images that instantiate mythological narratives, thereby facilitating reflective insight on and observation of underlying psychological processes. Recommendations are suggested for further investigation of the concepts explored in this study pertaining to specific populations that may benefit from nontraditional approaches to psychotherapy.
