TY  - THES
TI  - From Dracula to Dungeons & Dragons: Female Ideals across Time and Medium
AU  - Johansson, Jonathan
AB  - The Dungeons & Dragons module Ravenloft (1983) uses the novel Dracula (1897) to create a new story for a new format and while the texts are set during different timeperiods, they both respond in similar ways to the women’s movements of their time. Dracula corresponds to the rise of the New Woman in the 1800s and Ravenloft came out during what is described as a time of “backlash” against the women’s movement ofthe 1970s.

This essay uses this historical context and transmediation to compare and analyze the two works and show how they both use idealized female characters, with adaptation to their respective time periods, to impose limitations on female independence and autonomy. The ideals in both works are competent female characters whose safety and wellbeing are still predicated on their relationships to the men in theirlives. By contrast, female characters who strive for independence and power face punishment and death from the narrative. In this way both works espouse a singular ideal way to exist as a woman and that any step outside of this inevitably leads to hardship and suffering.
