TI  - Critical Roleplaying: An Exploration of Tabletop Role-Playing Games as Collaborative Fiction and the Roles of Players as Co-Authors of a Complex Narrative
AU  - Fox, Rachelle
T2  - Critical Role at the Florida College English Association’s annual conference in 2017 & the national College English Association’s annual conference in 2018
AB  - To better understand the phenomenon that is Critical Role, and to unravel how it and similar live-streamed RPGs have evolved as a form of entertainment media intended not just for players, but for an audience, I looked at several factors, including the nature of TTRPGs as collaborative storytelling and the relationship of live-streamed gameplay to other forms of entertainment, such as improv shows, sports broadcasts, and reality television. When examined all together, it becomes clear that there is something more to this entertainment medium than simply watching people play a game, and that the appeal of Critical Role and other live-streamed TTRPG programs owes much to the medium’s ability to leverage the effect of narrative transportation—that feeling of being drawn into a narrative through imagination and empathy for the characters—as well as the intensely collaborative nature of this form of storytelling, which requires participants to make and commit to highly consequential story-changing decisions on the fly. The willingness of players to commit to the “yes, and” of improv transforms the role-playing game into a powerful vehicle for collaborative storytelling, while the unpredictability of real-time dice rolls combined with the inversion of traditional pacing (such that fights that would take only seconds in reality play out over the course of hours) keep viewers and players alike on the edge of their seats for hours at a time, wondering what will happen next.
