TY  - JOUR
TI  - Keywords and Patterns in Role-Playing Game Online Discussion Forum
AU  - James, Robert Paul
AU  - Dykeman, Cass
AB  - Tabletop role-playing games (TRPGs) have been the topic of conversation and controversy since the inception of Dungeons &amp; Dragons™ in the 1970s. Misunderstandings about this unique group resulted in a long-term association of psychological dysfunction with this form of recreation that is still reflected in the views of mental health providers. The present study aimed to dispel some of these myths by using a corpus linguistics design to identify the unique keywords, multiterm words, and the word networks of the keywords that differentiate TRPG blogging from blogging in general. The keyness analysis identified the words “dnd” and “homebrew” as the two keywords and “bonus action” and “spell slot” as the multiword terms differentiating TRPG blogging from blogging in general. The word networks of dnd and homebrew demonstrate the unique linguistic patterns of this group as well as the focus on the communal nature of the TRPG experience. These results highlight the creative nature of TRPG play as well as the fact that this type of gaming focuses as much on the group experience as on any specific player. Counselors can use these findings to increase their cultural competence when working with TRPG players, as well as potentially harness their creative energy in the counseling process.
