AB  - This paper aims to investigate the different editorial strategies of the longest-running brazilian magazines focused on tabletop RPG games: Dragão Brasil (since 1994), and DragonSlayer (2005-2013). The popularity of board games evidences a context of editorial and economic changes that characterizes the convergence of the information and entertainment markets in contemporary times. Based on a case study (BRAGA, 2008), we seek to demonstrate how the editorial production, which started on the pages of the magazines, evolved into different independent products, in a transmedia context (JENKINS, 2015) that is sustained by crowdfunding.
