TY  - CHAP
TI  - Text-Based Role-Playing Games
AU  - Hammer, Jessica
AU  - Czege, Paul
T2  - The Routledge Handbook of Role-Playing Game Studies
AB  - This chapter describes text-based role-playing games in which players participate and interact via the medium of text. Text-based role-playing games involve a wide range of different designs and technologies to support play. For example, early text-based role-playing games were typically play-by-mail (PBM). Players wrote out their game moves and mailed them to a coordinator, who collated player contributions and responded in writing to the group. In contrast, contemporary text-based role-playing games focus on the process of writing, whether individually or collaboratively. This chapter presents two contemporary text-based role-playing game genres: the individual practice of journaling games and the collaborative practice of online freeform role-play. While these forms appear divergent on the surface, in effect they have three key common features: text (writing is a key part of play), trace (playing creates a physical or digital artifact), and temporality (they are typically played slowly and asynchronously). These features are important for understanding text-based role-playing games more broadly.
