TI  - The Shakespearean Ludisphere: Board Game Shakespeare
AU  - Smith, Corinn  Dakota Michael
AB  - This thesis explores connections between tabletop roleplaying games (TRPGs), Shakespearean adaptations, and the early modern theatrical stage. It is especially interested in the role of the player in a TRPG, which allows consumers of Shakespearean adaptations to embody the many roles of the player, actor, director, and playwright. As such, TRPGs and the role of the player allow for a unique form of narrative agency and engagement with Shakespeare unavailable in other mediums. This unique form of narrative agency, in addition to aspects of early modern theatrical practice like cross-dressing and single-sex casts, are brought together to examine the recuperative potential Shakespearean TRPGs offer transgender and queer players wishing to speak back to Shakespeare or establish a more positive presence in his – and our – world.
