TY  - CHAP
TI  - Chance in games
AU  - Caïra, Olivier
AU  - Chevalier, Sophie
T2  - Figures of Chance II
A2  - Duprat, Anne
A2  - James, Alison
AB  - The role of chance in games has evolved rapidly since the mid-twentieth century. Games rely not only on artefacts, designed to calibrate uncertainty, but also on psychological dispositions that allow us to engage with the action of the game despite its futility. This means that the choices that are made during play are guided not only by more or less random mechanisms (from chess to roulette) but also by how lucky we feel. Instead of pitting the agôn against the alea, most contemporary game designers mobilise chance in innovative ways: by randomising roles, environments or even rules; by uncoupling the notions of chance and luck or by using lot-drawing to build stories. Games and gambling create situations that reconcile tactical decision-making with moments of loss of control that occur when chance intervenes, as in horse-racing, for instance.
