TI  - 2 Rise of the wargaming Clubs
AU  - Peterson, Jon
T2  - Playing at the World, 2E: The Invention of Dungeons & Dragons
AB  - To understand the need to organize of American wargaming, one must first appreciate the scale of board wargaming as a hobby in the mid-1960s. It was, in a word, minuscule. In the first bimonthly issue of the Avalon Hill General in 1964, the subscriber's directory listed only seventy-two names, though as many as two thousand copies may have been printed for promotional purposes. The directory in the second issue boasted that subscriptions had leapt to over five hundred—evidently the promotion was a success. But the next two issues showed a more modest increase in subscriptions, under one hundred per issue, after which the practice of maintaining a directory was discontinued. Despite decent gains, wargaming was hardly a phenomenon holding millions in thrall.
