TI  - 9 The Fall of the IFW
AU  - Peterson, Jon
T2  - Playing at the World, 2E: The Invention of Dungeons & Dragons
AB  - In all of its incarnations, the monthly newsletter of the IFW—be it the Spartan of 1968, the IFW Monthly of 1969, or the semiprofessional International Wargamer thereafter—epitomized the potential of wargaming fandom. At the beginning of 1972, the newsletter reached the peak of its production quality and circulation. As a sounding board for some of the most important designs and designers of the preceding four years, the International Wargamer appeared to be an institution resting on a secure foundation. That illusion would be dashed in the spring of 1972, though the woes of the IFW extended back several months. The previous November, Lenard Lakofka, IFW President, had announced the resignation of both the Vice-President and Treasurer of the IFW, which meant that the responsibilities of both these offices reverted to the President.1 New elections were called for the IFW Senate over the Christmas holidays (Dave Arneson stood for a seat), but the returning instructions were unnecessarily complex, and as Don Lowry would remark as late as January 1973, "the results of election of officers held about a year ago have yet to be announced to the membership," leaving the work of the Senate in Lakofka's hands as well (PZF (57)).
