TY  - CHAP
TI  - 10 The Return of the Referee
AU  - Peterson, Jon
T2  - Playing at the World, 2E: The Invention of Dungeons & Dragons
AB  - To recap, in 1969, Arneson's Napoleonic Simulation Campaign had incorporated a postal Diplomacy game as a "background" strategic context for its tactical miniature battles, specifically the "Napoleonic Diplomacy II" variant by Gary Gygax. The Twin Cities gamers expanded the variant to include additional nations—ultimately comprising dozens of players participating in a variety of governmental capacities—more detailed management of national economic resources, and a healthy dose of Arneson's favorite: maritime action.1 When forces conflicted in the Diplomacy game, rather than resolving the disagreement of those colored blocks as the Diplomacy rules recommended, the contests were translated into miniature tabletop battles fought by the participating wargamers. While others had implemented such two-mode "strategy-tactical" wargames before, few dared to attempt them with so many geographically dispersed participants. This made the resolution of moves dependent upon the results and reporting of many miniature wargames conducted in remote locations, and turns could thus drag on for months. Over the course of nearly four real-world years of play, the game progressed through only six Diplomacy turns (which, in the variant in question, alternate between spring and fall), progressing in game time from the beginning of 1800 up to the end of 1802. Advancement at the strategic level held less importance for the participants than the tactical miniature battles. Naval combat followed the emerging Don't Give Up the Ship rules, but for warfare on land, the Twin Cities gamers invoked a different set of Napoleonic rules designed by local gamer Dave Wesely.
DA  - 2024///
PY  - 2024
