TI  - 12 The Fantasy Game
AU  - Peterson, Jon
T2  - Playing at the World, 2E: The Invention of Dungeons & Dragons
AB  - "The day after you left I began to feel a bit poorly, and by that night there was a sore area behind my left ear—mumps!" Gygax wrote back to Arneson in the first week of April. Over the intervening weeks, Gygax reported, "being sickly and not up to much, I drew up a couple levels of the local 'Blackmoor,' i.e. 'Greyhawk.'"1 In his first burst of creativity had drawn up twelve levels, and "some of my levels cover two sheets of paper." In the Great Kingdom, he reported the location of Greyhawk "will be that to the south, by the water, just below the Grand Duchy of Ernst (Urnst?) south of Nyr Div." This area was also then known as the "Wild Lands," though the land above it was wilder still, as Urnst was a prehistoric setting in the Great Kingdom, overrun with dinosaurs. To its west was Keoland—a place named for Gygax's deceased childhood friend Tom Keough—where there were beasts from Barsoom, the Martian setting of Edgar Rice Burroughs's novels, including thoats, banths, and apts. As Gygax began to develop rules, he drew on these sorts of exotic creatures for inspiration. But his focus was on the dungeon—already he could report that "the chaps were so nuts about the game that most of my spare time has flown into levels and rules for an eventual campaign similar to yours. We have 11 players at this moment!"
