TI  - 16 GenCon 1974 and Its Aftermath
AU  - Peterson, Jon
T2  - Playing at the World, 2E: The Invention of Dungeons & Dragons
AB  - While the previous six GenCons all marked pivotal points in this history, since each one afforded geographically dispersed gamers a rare opportunity to confer in person, GenCon VII (August 23-25, 1974) genuinely ushered in a new era. This transformation did not result from a vast increase in attendance, though with a headcount over 350, the seventh GenCon certainly crowded more gamers into the cramped Horticultural Hall than its predecessors.1 Nor does it rest on GenCon VII spilling over the bounds of the weekend to add a third day, Friday, to its schedule, given that impatient gamers had prematurely congregated on that day for some years. To GenCon VII belongs this: it introduced Dungeons & Dragons to the wargaming community. Gamers from around the country brought home rules, or at least news, of this novel game, and numerous wargaming clubs thereafter became increasingly—if not unhealthily—preoccupied with dungeon adventures. Fortunately, we have several detailed accounts of the goings-on at GenCon VII, including those of former IFW President Bill Hoyer in the American Wargamer and in Signal, and that of James Lur- vey in GPGPN (12), all published in September 1974.
