TI  - 20 The Summer Conventions of 1975
AU  - Peterson, Jon
T2  - Playing at the World, 2E: The Invention of Dungeons & Dragons
AB  - The first Origins convention in 1975 drew a truly nationwide audience, and perhaps no attendee better attested to the allure of the convention, and the culture of wargaming at the time, than Jack Greene, Jr. of the Simulations Design Corporation, publisher of Conflict magazine. Greene embarked on an epic coast-to-coast road trip between July 10 and August 3, 1975, to visit Origins and, along the way, to take the temperature of the national wargaming community.1 His write-up of this journey, entitled "My Pilgrimage to the Cathedrals of Wargaming," yields a great many insights into the reaction of the wargaming establishment to D&D (PZF (71)). Greene visited the three companies that he felt dominated the wargaming industry of the day: Game Designers Workshop (GDW), Avalon Hill, and Simulation Publications Incorporated (SPI).
