TI  - 21 The Bully Pulpit of Lake Geneva
AU  - Peterson, Jon
T2  - Playing at the World, 2E: The Invention of Dungeons & Dragons
AB  - TSR had, by the fall of 1975, enjoyed enough success that it could take a series of steps to cement its leadership of a small but rapidly growing segment of the hobby space. First, TSR consolidated its newly founded retail and mail order product business and its game development arm. Blume and Gygax used TSR Hobbies, Inc. as an umbrella company to purchase the assets of the original TSR partnership on September 26, 1975, effectively buying out Donna Kaye.1 From that point on, no longer were new TSR games emblazoned with the Gygax-Kaye "GK" logo, but instead with the lizard man of TSR Hobbies.2 In November 1975, consumer demand for Dungeons & Dragons justified a fourth printing of five thousand more copies; between January and November 1975, D&D shipped nearly three times as many boxed sets as it had during the same span the previous year. This latest printing abandoned the woodgrain box of prior editions in favor of a shrink-wrapped white box, and though it appeared in greater numbers, D&D sales still represented only a tiny fraction of the wargaming market. Sales do not necessarily reflect popularity, however, and this small Lake Geneva company grew at pace with its rising stature.
