TY  - JOUR
TI  - The Policy to Override Policies—One Policy to Rule Them All
AU  - Spellberg, Brad
T2  - JAMA Internal Medicine
AB  - In the role-playing game Dungeons &amp; Dragons (D&amp;D), which was considerably influenced by The Lord of the Rings, an epic fantasy novel in which a master ring is forged to rule all other magic rings, character personalities are created in 2 dimensions: (1) good, neutral, or evil and (2) lawful, neutral, or chaotic. Good vs neutral vs evil is fairly self-explanatory, but lawful vs neutral vs chaotic may be less so. D&amp;D characters with a lawful mentality believe that compliance with rules is itself the principal goal and that any deviation from rules is intrinsically wrong. In contrast, characters of a chaotic bent intrinsically believe all rules are wrong, period, and that they should be able to do whatever feels right. Those with a neutral alignment live in the middle. They have no intrinsic aversion to rules but believe that it is the spirit of the law that counts and that deviations from rigid adherence to rules make sense in some cases.
