TY  - THES
TI  - 探討 LARP 玩家遊戲表現、敘事傳輸和暫時擴張自我界限 對享樂感的影響 : The Effect of Gaming Performance, Narrative Transportation, Temporarily Expanding the Boundaries of the Self on the Enjoyment of LARP
AU  - Zhe, Chen
AB  - LARP (live action role playing) raised recently with the attraction of public and mass media, gaming performance of body movements, game script settings, role-playing are the major game characteristics. This study investigated the effect of gaming performance, narrative transportation, temporarily expanding the boundaries of the self (TEBOTS) on enjoyment, and the mediation effects of TEBOTS. Based on the data collected from the online survey of 404 LARP players in Taiwan, gaming performance, narrative transportation and TEBOTS predicted enjoyment significantly. Also, partial mediation of TEBOTS between gaming performance, narrative transportation and enjoyment was found. The results of this study are consistent with the previous research that there are correlations among narrative transportation, TEBOTS, and enjoyment in the massively multiplayer online role-playing game (MMORPG). In LARP games, the players' gaming performance is a significant contributor of enjoyment. Based on the results of post hoc analysis, young adults are the most frequent players; female players enjoy LARP more than male players. Less frequent (less than five times a year) players had lower scores on enjoyments, game performance, narrative transportation and TEBOTS; player with more than 2 types in comparison with single type revealed a similar result.
