TY  - JOUR
TI  - Lovecraft's Cosmic Horror in the German Tabletop Role-Playing Game FHTAGN
AU  - Wagner, Sandra Aline
T2  - Germanistik in Ireland: Yearbook of the German Studies Association of Ireland
AB  - H. P. Lovecraft (1890 – 1937), the most important American author of Weird Fiction, is known best for creating the Cthulhu mythos: an alternative universe, reigned by monstrous Gods, in which humanity eventually became meaningless. A contemporary audience can identify with the posthuman world depicted by Lovecraft; he is credited with inventing Cosmic Horror, a genre which makes explicit the insignificance of humanity within the vastness of space. It is within this dichotomy we find the horror in Lovecraft’s stories. The protagonists of Lovecraft’s stories are frequently struck by ‘Cosmic Fear’ after encountering a supernatural scenario, turning them into impotent and insane individuals. During past decades, various boardgames, videogames and tabletop role-playing games (TRPGs) have been trying to implement the characteristic of Cosmic Horror into their game play. In 2017, the Deutsche Lovecraft Gesellschaft e.V. created FHTAGN, the latest Lovecraftian TRPG in German. FHTAGN is named after a quote from Lovecraft’s Call of Cthulhu (“Ph'nglui mglw'nafh Cthulhu R'lyeh wgah'nagl fhtagn", which can be translated as "In his house at R'lyeh, dead Cthulhu waits dreaming.") FHTAGN is partly designed on the American TRPG Call of Cthulhu by Chaosium. Both games promise to rely heavily on Lovecraft’s concept of Cosmic Horror within the game’s story world(s), as well as in the ways in which creatures and rituals are handled in the game. This article will trace the extent to which the characteristic narrative structures of Lovecraft’s oeuvre were adapted in FHTAGN in order to outline the various possibilities and limitations of such a transmedia adaptation. For instance, it will examine how the powerlessness of the protagonist in Cthulhu’s universe subverts the agency of the player and how Cosmic Horror is implemented in example scenarios from FHTAGN.
