TY  - CHAP
TI  - Playing Across Planes with the D&D Multiverse
AU  - Alberto, Maria K.
T2  - Entering the Multiverse
A2  - Booth, Paul
AB  - Long after its initial release in 1974, the tabletop roleplaying game (TTRPG) Dungeons & Dragons (D&D) has undergone a cultural renaissance, created new commercial ties and partnerships, and revamped several of its classic titles. In this chapter I argue that its multiverse is another key part of how D&D is positioned and marketed. While official rulebooks and paratexts position the D&D multiverse as a means of enabling limitless possibility, in reality this particular conception of a multiverse functions to organize a variety of genres and other inspirations into one cohesive, salable product. The D&D multiverse – and its basis in planes of existence – thus functions as both a spatial schema within the fiction of this game system as well as an organizing schema for D&D as a commercial offering. To demonstrate, I delve into what the D&D multiverse is, where players encounter it, and what it is meant to achieve. I analyze texts, campaign settings, and concepts intricately tied to the D&D multiverse, and I also consider how this particular iteration of a multiverse is tied to its genre counterparts in other, more fixed media forms.
