TY  - THES
TI  - Facing Social Anxiety and Loneliness with Tabletop Roleplaying Games
AU  - Cleghorne, Brent W.
AB  - The purpose of the current study was to study if there is an effect between playing Tabletop Roleplaying Games (TTRPGs) and a person’s social anxiety and loneliness. It was the belief of the researcher that playing TTRPGs would lower people’s social anxiety and loneliness. A non-experimental research design method was used. 78 respondents were broken into two groups (plays TTRPGs and does not play TTRGPs) and asked to complete the Social Interaction Anxiety Scale (SIAS), the DeJong Gierveld Loneliness Scale Short Form (DJLS-SF), and questions related to playing habit for the TTRPG playing group. Statistical analysis examining the correlation between social anxiety and loneliness of TTRPG players and non-players, as well as player habits, found no significant correlation between playing TTRPGs and social anxiety and loneliness. Findings suggest that those who play Dungeons and Dragons are less likely to be lonely than those who prefer other games. Future research should focus on exploring the relationship between Dungeons and Dragons and potential mental health implications.
