TY  - THES
TI  - Embodied learning: utilizing rpgs for english language development
AU  - Martins, Igor Tolksdorf
AB  - The integration of Role-Playing Games (RPGs) into English language pedagogy represents a
novel approach grounded in contemporary cognitive science theories. Traditional language
acquisition models, predominantly influenced by Cartesian perspectives, have long emphasized
internal cognitive processes, treating language learning as a mental activity confined to the
brain. These models often prioritize the internalization of grammatical rules and vocabulary
through structured exercises, overlooking the dynamic and context-dependent nature of real-
world language use. This study challenges such traditional approaches by adopting the 4E
Cognition framework – embodiment, embeddedness, enactivism, and the extended mind –
which affirms that cognitive processes, including language acquisition, are deeply intertwined
with bodily actions, environmental contexts, and social interactions. The research is anchored
in qualitative observations conducted within the framework of an “RPG Club” project at an
English school, where students participated in Dungeons & Dragons (D&D) sessions as a means
of enhancing their English language skills. By focusing on the principles of Non-Cartesian
Cognitive Science, the study examines how RPGs, as immersive and interactive learning tools,
can foster authentic language use, critical thinking, creativity, and collaborative learning in
contextually rich environments. The RPG sessions provided students with opportunities to
engage in meaningful communication, negotiate meaning, and solve problems through
language in ways that mirror real-life interactions. Through detailed observations of student
interactions, language use, and engagement during RPG sessions, the study reveals the benefits
of RPGs in promoting linguistic proficiency and cognitive development. This research
contributes to the growing body of literature on innovative language learning methodologies by
demonstrating the potential of RPGs as powerful tools for teaching English as an additional
language. The pedagogical implications of this research offer educators a robust framework for
improving language acquisition through experiential learning and active engagement with
meaningful communication in immersive environments.
