TY  - BOOK
TI  - Utopia on the Tabletop
AU  - Walton, Jo Lindsay
AB  - Utopia on the Tabletop unites the venerable field of Utopian Studies with the relatively new field of Tabletop Roleplaying Game Studies (TTRPG Studies). The main chapters fall loosely into three sections. In the first section are six chapters each focusing on a specific tabletop roleplaying game, tracing utopian (and other -topian) energies. The irrepressible Dungeons & Dragons snorts and stamps in the middle section, with three chapters exploring official Wizards of the Coast publications, a significant homebrew supplement, and an actual play podcast. Then come five mostly shorter chapters with greater emphasis on practitioner reflection. Dialogues follow: Siân Adiseshiah on utopian drama, Doug Geisler on roleplaying union organising, Kellynn Wee on speculative anthropology, and Gabriel Caetano Barbosa on game design, facilitation, decolonisation, and more. There are also a pair of small, fully playable roleplaying games, by Paul Czege and Gabriel Caetano Barbosa.1 How to Ooze Charm in the Future is oriented to solo play, and Roots & Flowers for a group. Finally there is an afterword by me, suggesting some broad links between TTRPGs and utopia studies, and proposing a kind of defence of the blueprint utopia.
