TY  - CHAP
TI  - Games of Personal Horror Shadow Work and Role playing Experiences Sarah Lynne Bowman
AU  - Bowman, Sarah Lynne
T2  - Monstrosity in Games and Play: A Multidisciplinary Examination of the Monstrous in Contemporary Cultures
A2  - Stang, Sarah
A2  - Meriläinen, Mikko
A2  - Blom, Joleen
A2  - Hassan, Lobna
AB  - This chapter discusses the use of non-digital role-playing games to explore monstrous aspects of
the personal and collective unconscious, called the Shadow. This theoretical framework views
selfhood as psychodynamic, composed of multiple interrelated parts, the most monstrous of
which often remain unconscious until permitted to emerge through play. Thus, play can drive
personal and social development through Shadow work, the processing of these play experiences.
Drawing from 13 expert interviews, five categories of role-playing experiences conducive to
Shadow work are presented: 1) experiencing loss, violation, and/or trauma; 2) exerting power
over others; 3) portraying undesirable personality traits; 4) expressing mental health challenges;
and 5) exploring dysfunctional or maladaptive social dynamics. The chapter concludes with
ethical considerations inherent to conducting Shadow work.
