TY  - JOUR
TI  - Haunted Time in New York by Night
AU  - Wilcox, EA
T2  - JCMS: Journal of Cinema and Media Studies
AB  - This essay discusses the actual play show New York by Night (NYBN; Twitch, World of Darkness, 2022–) and how it evokes the disjointed temporality of the HIV/AIDS crisis in New York City, both before and after highly active antiretroviral therapy (HAART), without a specific HIV/AIDS storyline.1 Set in the contemporary 2020s, NYBN embodies the frustrating tension between "epidemic" (pre-1997) and "endemic" (1997–) time—terms Jules Gill-Peterson defines to differentiate the immediate urgency in HIV/AIDS activism pre-HAART and the gradual adjustment to "calculated" slow death post-HAART.2 The hazy slippage between periods produces a "frustrating sense of belatedness" for those born or coming-of-age in the endemic era, as if arriving too late to help in any meaningful way.3 I look to NYBN because its vampires literalize meeting the "ghosts produced by the inheritances of the epidemic" and because it collapses the space between present and epidemic times of the AIDS crisis through cinematic landscape.4 In so doing, the show exemplifies how actual plays can evoke a productive intimacy with the past through improvised performance bound by structures devised in specific historical and cultural moments.  

The show, despite using the fifth edition of Vampire: The Masquerade (VTM; White Wolf Publishing, 2018) to structure its performance, wrestles with how vampires are still seen as transmission vectors and the ways these creatures engender fear and cement the game in the "epidemic" era of its very first edition (White Wolf Publishing, 1991).5 First edition VTM references the Centers for Disease Control (CDC) explicitly, stating that the CDC worried "that the virus [HIV] no longer requires sexual contact to be spread … [and] they have begun an enormous investigation."6 These HIV-infected vampires imbricate the HIV/AIDS crisis, governmental surveillance via the CDC, and potential exposure of vampiric existence. This game design replicates real-world suspicions of people with HIV/AIDS because these HIV-infected vampires become community threats rather than community members. Subsequent editions of VTM (White Wolf Publishing, 1992, 1998; Crowd Control Productions, 2011) retain and build on this threat model; however, fifth edition VTM attempts to veer away from demonizing specific diseases, using instead the broader "Dangers to the Blood" game mechanic.7 This mechanic states that "should they [the vampire] contract a disease from infected human blood, they pass it on to the kine [humans] they feed upon, while the germs die in their own system."8 Despite eliminating game mechanics that directly position HIV-positive vampires as threats exposing the global network used to hide vampires from human existence, called "the Masquerade," the fifth edition edits cannot eliminate the foundational influence the HIV/AIDS crisis has had on the VTM game system itself.
