TY  - JOUR
TI  - Coming Out in the Bloom: Identity, Play, and Playing with Identity in A Court of Fey & Flowers
AU  - Hedge, Stephanie
T2  - JCMS: Journal of Cinema and Media Studies
AB  - In episode 4 of Dimension 20's A Court of Fey & Flowers (Dropout, 2022), ethereal fae and Mistrex of the Bloom Delloso de la Rue (they/them), played by Oscar Montoya, drops their glamour, or magical disguise, to reveal that their true form is a monstrous owlbear—a move within gameplay that Montoya has likened to an LGBTQ+ coming out. Existing in a world defined by appearances and reputation, Rue's story arc throughout the season follows this coming out experience and their search for authenticity and truth. A theme emerging in A Court of Fey & Flowers (ACOFAF) is one of self-acceptance as the pathway to love and the rejection of a society that requires repression of the self—here, filtered through tabletop roleplaying features like glamour and reputation trackers. There are three compelling layers to Rue as a character: the person they are showing to the world; the authentic owlbear body and identity that lies underneath; and the living, human person, Montoya, a queer man playing the character. Notably, Rue's story is driven by and told through Montoya's body as he plays at the table in front of a camera. This essay offers a close reading of ACOFAF centered on the story of Rue's coming out—a gameplay move that enables an exploration of the intersections [End Page 180] between narrative, gameplay mechanics, and actual play performance from a queer perspective. Through this example, I argue that actual play shows afford a space to play with identity—and, ultimately, for that play to imagine a hopeful outcome.

Dimension 20 is an actual play show, one of over twenty produced by the streaming service Dropout. ACOFAF is the fifteenth season of the show and features guest game master (GM) Aabria Iyengar guiding her "pack of pixies" through a story of high society—an "Austen-esque Period-Drama in a Fey [fairy] setting," according to the Dimension 20 (Dropout, 2018–) fanwiki.1 The ten episodes of ACOFAF aired weekly on Dropout between August and October 2022 and used Dungeons & Dragons 5e (Wizards of the Coast, 2014) as the core game system, supplemented by game mechanics from Good Society: A Jane Austen RPG (Storybrewers Roleplaying, 2018), a regency roleplaying game that includes reputation, rumor, and letter mechanics—a combination that created unique narrative possibilities.
