TY  - JOUR
TI  - Chosen Structures of Belonging: Kith and Kin in Actual Play
AU  - Burton, Adrianna
T2  - JCMS: Journal of Cinema and Media Studies
AB  - Two of the most popular actual plays (AP) from the beginning of the tabletop roleplaying game (TTRPG) resurgence over the last several years are Critical Role (Geek & Sundry, 2015–2018; Critical Role Productions, 2018–) and The Adventure Zone (Maximum Fun, 2014–).1 Notably, both APs feature a set of twins: queer, out-of-place orphans who stumble into an adventuring party—a new family. By choosing to feature family as a central theme, both shows have set trends for the importance of family in AP writ large. This essay argues that the twins in both APs embody the uniquely queer experience of finding family at a TTRPG table.

Critical Role and The Adventure Zone demonstrate explicit investment in queer representation: Vax'ildan (Vax) and Vex'ahlia (Vex) from the first campaign of Critical Role are bisexual, whereas in The Adventure Zone, Taako is gay and Lup is established as trans offscreen. Both twins create family within their adventuring party—that is, with the other player characters—and renegotiate their relationship with each other. To explore this, I consider the anthropological theory of queer kinship and connect this to queer game studies' exploration of futurity and worldbuilding. By drawing together conversations around queer kin in these ways, I argue that these siblings emblematize active queer worldbuilding through the reconceptualization of family and kinship. Specifically, they arrive at a kind of queer kinship, which, as Kath Weston explains, positions queerness as the basis for a new type of family structure.2 By applying queer kinship to AP shows and characters, I show how queer worldmaking is at the heart of the TTRPG table, distinctive for its underlying investment in queer kinship within an adventuring party.

Across both shows, the twins represent distinct kinship practices. While kinship has been discussed at length in anthropology, particularly of interest to this work is the subset of literature on queer kinship. As Weston demonstrates after years of fieldwork, distinctly queer kinship features more sibling-sibling and (queer) spousal relations than parent-child and hetero-normative spousal relations; that is, when looking for queer kinship practices in AP, there is likely a skewed focus toward sibling-based relationships.3 In Critical Role, the relationship between Vex and Vax aligns with two of three common positions in kinship practices: parent-child and sibling positions. The third position is that of a spousal relationship, which comes into play for the twins in both APs. As Dwight Read et al. explain, positions and terminologies vary widely across cultures, but kinship is generally understood genealogically.
