TY  - JOUR
TI  - Matters in the Bloom: Material Ecocriticism in Dimension 20's Actual Play A Court of Fey & Flowers
AU  - Baker, Sylvan
T2  - JCMS: Journal of Cinema and Media Studies
AB  - Dimension 20 (2018–), created by the production company Dropout, is an actual play series where actors produce and engage with narratives through a tabletop roleplaying game (TTRPG) system, facilitated by a game master (GM)—sometimes referred to as a dungeon master (DM)—for audience entertainment. Overall, the Dimension 20 series has used several different TTRPGs while creating their content, such as Dungeons & Dragons 5e (Wizards of the Coast, 2014), Kids on Bikes (Renegade Game Studios, 2019) and related system Kids on Brooms (Renegade Game Studios, 2022), and Good Society: A Jane Austen RPG (Storybrewers Roleplaying, 2018), each with varying mechanics that can determine the gameplay and interaction between the actors, the characters, and the storied worlds. A certain connective vibrancy exists in the immersion of the actors with their characters, as well as the audience consumption of each season.1 This liveliness does not stop at the stories between characters but traverses the realms depicted in the storied spaces, encompasses the worlds of the people at the table, and moves beyond the screen to our own realities. 

In particular, Dimension 20's season A Court of Fey & Flowers (ACOFAF), airing from August through October 2022 with ten episodes averaging around two hours each, represents material story subjects of gameplay and the actors' world at the table. GM Aabria Iyengar incorporates into her storytelling items called tokens, a Good Society mechanic that uses objects of importance to interact with the game's narrative, which are physically brought to the table as she matches them to an actor's character decisions. Iyengar invites players and audiences to join in a material discourse; her blending of tabletop systems Dungeons & Dragons and Good Society permits objects to hold power over the story as much as the actors do. These objects create opportunities for lively "intra-actions"—connections between humans and nonhumans acting on/with/in one another in entangled and agentic ways that implicate storied and real worlds at the table and in our everyday lives.2 These agencies are made visible between the fey members of the Bloom, Iyengar's nod toward the London social seasons, and their relationships and the things with which they intra-act. One character in particular who highlights this dynamic at the table and on the screen is Captain K. P. Hob, played by Brennan Lee Mulligan. Hob reveals the interweaving of human and nonhuman matters through his habit of consuming things that matter to him.

For this essay, I focus on one moment of player-character intra-action with a peacock feather in order to reveal the power of things that hail audiences toward their own agentic material liveliness.3 It is important to note that throughout the narrative, Hob, among other characters, collects trash and treasures in ways that might leave the audience rethinking how performers and spectators interact with other storied environments. Mulligan, through K. P. Hob, exemplifies the notion that, even in an actual play, material life extends beyond the objects that we produce or use. In fact, we are constantly and intricately connected to the materials around us. I draw on ecocriticism to analyze Mulligan's consumptive performance with, through, around, and against these things—such as the tokens of the game and the letters exchanged between characters—as well as his other material performances with items that are already a part of tabletop gaming systems. Through this material ecocritical reading, we can envision how ACOFAF and, more broadly, tabletop roleplaying raise significant questions about the connections we humans have with our ecological world. Actual plays might offer an alternate and more inclusive way of digesting and transforming information between ecological, synthetic, and real realms by highlighting the world's connectivity.
