TY  - JOUR
TI  - "Class Conflict": Dimension 20: Misfits and Magic and TTRPGs as Transformative Fantasy
AU  - French, Emma
T2  - JCMS: Journal of Cinema and Media Studies
AB  - Dimension 20: Misfits and Magic (Dropout, 2021) is an actual play show that exemplifies how tabletop roleplaying games (TTRPGs) encourage transformative, subversive readings of the fantasy canon and how actual play media give these interpretations greater impact within what Helen Young terms "the Fantasy genre-culture."1 This four-episode Dimension 20 mini-series, aired on Dropout.tv and led by game master Aabria Iyengar, originates in the Kids on Brooms (Renegade Game Studios, 2020) system, a TTRPG that helps players create "magical school" stories using the conventions of J. K. Rowling's Harry Potter franchise.2 The Misfits and Magic cast (Brennan Lee Mulligan, Erika Ishii, Danielle Radford, and Lou Wilson) use this game text to critique Rowling's work, in light of her conservative political views and the mounting opposition to her work by popular fandom. Rather than facilitating imitation, TTRPGs allow players to respond critically to texts, revising them through play. 

This essay uses Misfits and Magic to illustrate how TTRPGs facilitate critical relationships to fantasy genre-culture. Misfits and Magic utilizes Kids on Brooms to emphasize the Anglocentric, colonial underpinnings of the magic school subgenre. Actual play moves these once-private responses into the public eye, thus offering a new space to generate revisionist perspectives on fantasy. TTRPGs traditionally have complex relationships to the fantasy texts they consciously adapt, or that inspired them. As a participatory practice, TTRPGs intensify preexisting relationships with fantasy; most TTRPG players encounter fantasy in other media first. Daniel Mackay argues that Dungeons & Dragons (TSR, 1974–1997; Wizards of the Coast, 1997–) "players draw … from the cultural sphere," assembling their characters from "vivid traces from literary passages or film scenes," intertextual references, and "decontextualized tropes" known as "fictive blocks" in their storytelling.3

TTRPGs are a self-reflexive part of Young's fantasy genre-culture. Young defines genre-culture as the "textual practices [of fantasy, situated] within a wider set of social processes that include not only Fantasy conventions, but the behaviours of authors and audiences, the ideological arguments that circulate around the texts, and the meaning and location of Fantasy within a political economy."4 TTRPGs encapsulate all of this definition. Using a social process, players reflect upon authorial texts, audience reactions, and key discourses within the genre. TTRPGs give fans transformative agency, either to take ownership of texts for which they have a strong affective relationship or to amend them to accommodate individual experiences—perhaps reflecting what players wish to see within fantasy. While play is not always performed with subversive intent, it contains subversive potential. When condensing fantasy—or a fantasy subgenre—into a rubric, unspoken textual trends become legible rules. Players are given a set of dominant textual practices they can choose to affirm or break. Some playful choices—for instance, the decision to affirm traditional representations of race within fantasy, or to align oneself with a popular author's exclusionary politics—may also constitute a political stance within genre-culture.
