TY  - THES
TI  - L’impact de la narration de jeu de rôle éducatif sur la motivation des apprenants
AU  - Algayres, Muriel
AB  - Finding strategies to support students’ motivation and commitment to the task is a well-known issue for teacher, and a major subject for scientific research in the field of cognitive psychology. In this context, serious games and educational games are a tool which, in spite of a growing interest, remains largely untapped. Through the analysis of quantitative data gathered from high school students, we tried to establish whether students engaged in educational roleplaying game’s narration could present a better self-determination rate and average intrinsic motivation scores than students from a control group. The results showed that students engaged in a narrative and playful activity present a more important increase of these scores than students of the control group. They invite us to consider in a positive light the role of narration and educational games as didactic tools, and to envisage the development of new tools that would allow us to reinforce the positive impact of narration on learners.
