TY  - THES
TI  - Playing to Create Ourselves: Exploring Larp and Visual Autoethnographic Practice as a Tool of Self Liberation for Marginalized Identities
AU  - Kemper, Jonaya D.
AB  - This thesis uses visual autoethnography, critical race theory, applied theater practices, and trauma studies to explore how the co-created medium of larp, or live action roleplaying, allows marginalized people to achieve a sense of self liberation through play, participation, and reflexive writing. In this context self liberation has two meanings. The first is the situational awareness of the impact of systemic oppression which effects the members of marginalized community. The second is the feeling that a marginalized individual has the power to break free of internalized oppressions that uphold their structural oppression and hinder them in their own personal lives. I argue that larping, like theater, has the ability to allow its participants a unique way to share their stories and investigate society through its lens by allowing them to try on different selves and identities. For players who suffer from structural oppression, this thesis maintains that larp can provide an avenue to decolonize their minds and bodies from internalized oppressions and provide a narrative in which they can see themselves outside of their oppression or conquering their oppression. The study explores larp as a medium that facilitates identity alteration and suggests a visual autoethnographic method unique to larp. This method allows the participant to fix the otherwise ephemeral medium of larp to a personal narrative that can be dissected for participant self exploration. The liminal feelings between self and character after a larp, commonly called bleed, are fleeting. As such, any self-liberatory effects of that bleed like awareness of internalized oppression, which this thesis defines as emancipatory bleed, are also fleeting, it is necessary to create a record of that experience in order to investigate the self in relation to the character narrative. Furthermore, this paper dissects the pitfalls that stand between larp and its ability to be a liberatory medium and suggests that in order for larp to grow into its potential as a liberatory medium, it must become intersectional, (here defined as considering the intertwined oppressions of ethnicity, class, gender, sexuality and ability) in design, practice, and player base.
