TY  - JOUR
TI  - “A Beast I Am, Lest a Beast I Become:” Tabletop Role-Playing Games, Vampires and the Questioning of Power
AU  - Ray, Jean-Charles
T2  - Scaffold: Journal for the Comparative Studies of Literature, Art and Culture
AB  - Bram Stoker’s late-Victorian gothic take on the figure of the vampire revealed many
ambiguous facets regarding both the character’s identity and his position toward
society, and opened up new avenues for interpretation, appropriation and
prolongation: His Dracula is both a menacing figure from the margins of the West,
invading the heart of the British Empire (leading to Nosferatu, Murnau, 1922) and a
defender of Christianity, who became monstrous by confronting the foreign enemy
(particularly visible in Dracula Untold, Shore, 2014). He is a nocturnal predator
seducing women (Blood of Dracula, Strock, 1957), but also an agent supporting
female emancipation oppressed by patriarchal and religious yoke (Coppola’s
romantic vision in 1992). Such an interpretative variety reveals a deeper challenge:
one toward the storyteller, their identity and authority. Dracula never tells the story
directly but drives the novel’s narrative between the texts. Since these movies and
novels have already been amply commented on, in a sense reproducing the epistolary
framing of the vampire in Dracula, this text focuses on an area that has been little
explored to date: the vampire in the tabletop role-playing game. In this medium, the
vampire has taken on extremely diverse identities, some of which have been little
analyzed. From Dungeons & Dragons (Gygax and Arneson, 1974) to Vampire: The
Masquerade (Rein-Hagen, 1991), the vampire, in turn, took on traits of the nemesis
to slay, the power fantasy, rebellion against the established order and lyrical, tragic
romanticism. But much of this evolution has taken place in the shadow of these big
names in role-playing, in games such as Creature Feature (Acres, 1986), Prédateurs
(Chouvel et al., 1990) and Nightlife (McDevitt, 1990). Moreover, while the fifth
edition of Vampire: The Masquerade has been flourishing since 2018, it should not
overshadow the many prolific independent productions, from Svart av Kval, Vit av
Lust (Pettersson, 2019) to Eat the Reich (Howitt, 2023). This article will examine the
evolution of the vampire figure in tabletop role-playing from 1974 to 2024. Such a
period obviously does not allow for claims of exhaustivity. Still, discussing a few
cases that have marked the medium’s history can account for the relationship between this figure and the development of role-playing’s means of expression, on
the one hand, and cultural and social transformations, on the other. From adversary to
protagonist, from bloodthirsty monster to sensitive being, the vampire expanded the
medium’s expressive possibilities, notably by questioning who gets to tell the story.
As the titular figure of Buffy the Vampire Slayer proclaimed (season 7, episode 1),
"It’s about power," and as a figure, the vampire invites us to rethink its use and
distribution, especially regarding the narrative.
