TY  - ENCYC
TI  - Golem (Jewish folklore)
AU  - Kalso, Reed
T2  - EBSCO Research Starters
AB  - The golem is a mythological creature from Jewish folklore, commonly depicted as a humanoid figure created from clay or stone by a human creator, most famously Rabbi Judah Loew ben Bezalel in 16th century Prague. The golem is initially under the control of its creator and serves to protect the Jewish community from perceived threats, but various stories illustrate how it can become unruly, sometimes leading to unintended consequences due to its literal interpretation of commands. The narrative raises philosophical and ethical questions about free will, the creator-creation relationship, and the morality of controlling another being. The origin of the golem parallels Biblical creation stories, particularly the formation of Adam from earth, and has influenced numerous artistic expressions and modern fictional works, including Mary Shelley's "Frankenstein." The golem remains a significant cultural symbol in the Czech Republic and has inspired various interpretations in media, from films to role-playing games. Despite its enduring popularity, there are concerns from Jewish leaders regarding the misrepresentation of the golem's significance in broader contexts. The legend serves as a reminder of the complexities of creation and the potential consequences of human ambition.
