TY  - CHAP
TI  - Historicising the Roguelite: The Legacy of Neoliberalism and the Dawn of Social Media
AU  - Harvie, Steven
T2  - The Rise of the Roguelite
AB  - Eli Cook argued that, through the gameplay of narrative decision, the popular 1980s ‘Choose Your Own Adventure’ (CYOA) books accommodated implicit ideological messages that mirrored and reinforced the reigning neoliberal values of the latter half of the 20th century; by rewarding risk and punishing the cautious the CYOA books championed the enterprising neoliberal subject. Building on Cook's analysis, this chapter argues that the core roguelite mechanics of risk, chance, and randomness – features retained from its 1980s ancestor, the roguelike – are similarly embroiled in the ideology of neoliberal economics. But roguelites are also informed by a social context radically different from the 1980s. Social media, for example, has fundamentally changed how we interact with the world and each other, and has evolved itself from a series of social networking websites to an intensely gamified and algorithm-driven set of media platforms. Drawing on parallels between social media and roguelite design, I argue that these seemingly innocuous games are entangled within a cultural politics that informs our understanding of the world. The popularity of roguelites is therefore no accident; the roguelite thrives today because it fits seamlessly within a digital environment that prioritises randomness, quantifiability, instant rewards (and punishments).
