TY  - CHAP
TI  - Introduction: The Abcanny: Encounters With the Inscrutably Alien
AU  - Daniels, Aaron B.
T2  - A Phenomenology of the Alien
AB  - Framed with a visit to an aging movie palace, Daniels contrasts the overflowing symbols and tropes that dominate Star Wars (Lucas, 1977) to the cryptic imagery of Kubric’s 2001: A Space Odyssey (1968). Interrogating “mystery,” the author outlines three levels—puzzles and riddles, Life Mysteries, and Ultimate Mysteries—offering fictional and clinical examples for each. This leads to an examination of science fiction and horror films from the last 50 years, offering viewers encounters with various forms of the inscrutably alien. Daniels asserts that the film Annihilation (Garland, 2018), based on the Weird fiction of VanderMeer, integrates most of the themes present in the preceding examples. Daniels then contrasts Freud’s and Heidegger’s uncanny and Kristeva’s abject. In all cases, some form of essentialism seems to be at work, or as Miéville describes it, “the unknown known.” Thus, Daniels describes Miéville’s abcanny: that which not only does not mean anything but also undoes meaning itself. Weird fiction authors Lovecraft and Ligotti round out the discussion. Facing these dark and potentially nihilistic perspectives, Daniels notes that these authors still have the audacity to create. Therefore, Daniels concludes with a playful neologism—the shabcanny, as seen in the creativity of the Dark Cabaret.
