TY  - CONF
TI  - No Dungeon Masters Please, We’re British: Britain's Game Design Revolution
AU  - Apperley, Thomas
AU  - Sturrock, Ian
T2  - What is British games research?
AB  - How UK game designers reimagined play by ditching the GM and rewriting the rules of the hobby. From 1970s hobbyists to global markets: our UK Game Scene project uncovers how British designers reshaped tabletop and roleplaying games through innovation and infrastructure. The “GM problem” was a bottleneck. British designers cracked it by making play modular, solo-friendly, and widely accessible—long before mainstream digital design caught up. Games Workshop went from importing D&D to crafting Doctor Who and Judge Dredd games. When TSR UK landed, GW doubled down on homegrown IP. While TSR pushed TV shows and novels, GW built Citadel Miniatures, launched gamebooks, and published Warhammer. The UK scene scaled by blending formats. GMs filtered access to RPGs—socially, financially, geographically. UK designers tackled this head-on, making games playable anywhere, by anyone. Solving the GM problem meant redefining what games were and who played them. It was a cultural transformation. GW’s 1980 boardgames (like Valley of the Four Winds) broke new ground: immersive, GM-free, and expandable. Talisman proved players didn’t need a master to have "fun." Fighting Fantasy gamebooks (1982–) merged dice with narrative, ditching the GM and going mainstream via Puffin Books. 20 million sold = a solo revolution. Warlock (1984–86) made FF gamebooks part of newsagent culture—serialized play, mass accessibility. Warhammer (1983) was GW’s most effective GM workaround: expandable, replayable, and built for skirmish. Critics scoffed at its RPG rules, but the empire grew. Too rural for a game group? Play-by-mail filled the gap. KJC’s hand-moderated systems delivered RPG vibes through the post—proto-MMO before modems. 1980s UK computer games (e.g. Sphinx Adventure) brought D&D themes to solo screens. Early IF met education tech—fantasy without a GM, just a BBC Micro. Bartle’s MUD1 turned TRPG logic into real-time multiplayer. GW noticed but missed the model. MUDs were digital dungeons without masters. Reframing TRPGs meant lowering the bar: solo modes, bookstores, asynchronous play. Books and mail—not rulebooks and sessions—were the new entry points. UK designers cross-bred boardgames, RPGs, wargames, and computers. The result? Flexible, stylish, and culturally distinct IPs that still shape play today. Solo play. Grimdark. Transmedia. GM-less models. The UK scene’s innovations weren’t just local—they set templates for global, inclusive gaming futures.
