TY  - THES
TI  - The Role of Krashen’s Affective Filter Hypothesis on Language Learning Effectiveness, Teacher Training and Professional Development
AU  - Gidman, Harvey Harwar
AB  - The objective of this paper is to compile my professional experiences,
development and reflections on my 27 years as an English teacher and coordinator. I have
contextualized my background in other fields, my culture and significant events in my
teaching career. I emphasized the most important aspects of educational philosophies
such as the Affective Filter Hypothesis, CALLA/CLIL, and Multiple Intelligence Theory
that shaped my classroom management and coordination. Recent events such as COVID
caused me to reflect on the changing roles of technology and digital assistance as an
educator, so I conducted a SWOT analysis of my current professional formation. I
identified my experience; my dedication to continuing education through my B.A.
Education and my Licencitura; and my commitment to engaging and using current
educational methodologies in my classroom and planning. Taking advantage of the
SWOT analysis also led me to reflect and then develop a meaningful six-month program
to enhance my digital development, classroom management, and relationship with my
school’s community. This will be done through in school technology classes, joining
online English teacher support groups and starting an online Spanish course, while
maintaining a coach and agenda. These reflections and activities have had a positive
impact on myself as a teacher and coordinator and improve my value to my school
community and employment marketplace. I also believe I have much to give to other
teachers as a mentor upon the self-reflection of this work.
