TY  - JOUR
TI  - Transcultural Learning with Role-Playing Games: Awareness Through First-Person Experiences
AU  - Kamm, Björn-Ole
T2  - Mechademia
AB  - Nondigital role-playing games (RPGs) within and outside Japan continue to draw academic attention as entertainment. Simultaneously, they are being utilized for educational and therapeutic purposes. This article explores how live-action role-playing (larp) can convey complex social issues, focusing on minority challenges in mainstream society. The primary aim was to design a larp that fosters transcultural learning about the experiences and societal hurdles faced by neuro-divergent individuals (e.g., autistics), promoting empathy and awareness among neurotypical participants.

Building on the concepts of "Nordic larp" and performance ethnography, we collaborated with neuro-divergent informants and educators to design a larp that metaphorically represents the everyday challenges of autism. The larp, set in a science-fiction context, includes nonlinear play and metaphorical elements to simulate sensory overload, energy depletion, and social misunderstandings. The design process emphasized participant safety, so the larp involves pre-game workshops and post-game debriefings to facilitate reflection and learning.

The larp, titled Sirius—11F84—Colors of Light, was successfully run in both Germany and Japan, with participants reporting enhanced understanding and empathy towards neurodivergent experiences. The structured debriefing, supported by the "reLarp" app, revealed significant themes of communication challenges and the duality of freedom and restriction within the gameplay. Feedback highlighted the educational value and the experimental nature of the larp design, suggesting its potential as a powerful tool in academic communication and social awareness. The chapter demonstrates that larps can effectively translate complex social science research into immersive experiences, fostering empathy and transcultural understanding through first-person engagement.
