TY  - THES
TI  - Playing a Dangerous Game: Games and the Development of Stereotypes in Moral Panics From 1976-1999
AU  - Burton, Joseph M .
AB  - Beginning in the 1970s, with the advent of commercially available and accessible video games in North America, games went from being trivial and relatively innocuous elements of childhood culture to major touchstones of North American mass culture. Games were no longer just the domain of children and often came to symbolize the threats and dangers of a rapidly shifting technological and cultural landscape. This led to a series of moral panics that were centered upon these new, often complex and increasingly realistic games that were apparently a source of moral corruption for children and teenagers. This view of games as a moral hazard for young people was often taken up by the news and mass media and opened the path for many moral entrepreneurs to leverage ‘common sense’ Media Effects thinking and gamer stereotypes for their own personal gain. These elements became common themes that persisted through each of the moral panics, so much so that by 1999 video games themselves were implicated in the tragedy of the Columbine Massacre. Thus, by 1999, video games were certainly no longer merely the domain of children, but understood to be one of the most powerful, and dangerous aspects of the modern world.
