TY  - CHAP
TI  - The Warhammer Product and Service Portfolio: Wayfinding Information as Competitive Advantage
AU  - Baker, Neal
T2  - Navigating Imaginary Worlds: Wayfinding and Subcreation
A2  - Wolf, Mark J. P.
AB  - This chapter covers the Warhammer imaginary worlds product portfolio of Games Workshop (GW). An expanding range of products helps customers navigate two universes that form the company's intellectual property—a science fiction setting for tabletop miniature games and licensed derivatives (Warhammer 40,000, Warhammer The Horus Heresy, and Necromunda) and a fantasy setting that likewise supports gaming (Warhammer Age of Sigmar and Blood Bowl). This chapter analyzes core products like rulebooks and army guidebooks to highlight a corporate strategy that mixes diegetic and extradiegetic wayfinding and examines a similar wayfinding mix on GW's “Warhammer Community” website. The chapter then explores the ramifications of two GW wayfinding services—Warhammer+ subscriber TV content and the Warhammer Alliance educational program for schools, public libraries, and youth scouting groups. It concludes by suggesting that GW's 500+ retail stores serve as navigation portals to the imaginary worlds of Warhammer. The upshot is that GW's wayfinding product portfolio supplies a competitive advantage for gamers and hobbyists engaging with Warhammer imaginary worlds while at the same time offering the company a competitive advantage in the “real world” marketplace.
CY  - New York
DA  - 2025///
PY  - 2025
