TY  - THES
TI  - Satanism! Subversion! Suicide! Christian Evangelical Imaginations Regarding Dungeons & Dragons, 1979-1991
AU  - Olsson, Adam
AB  - The overarching purpose of this essay is to create a deeper and more holistic understanding of how Dungeons & Dragons was imagined by U.S. American Christian evangelical actors within
the context of the satanic panic of the 1980s. These imaginations are analysed using discourse analysis. For the discourse analysis, the trouble and problematisation model will be utilised. To
achieve the goal, a variety of written material produced by Christian evangelical actors between 1979 and 1991 is examined. This material includes books, pamphlets, and tracts.
My main, overarching question is thus as follows:
● How was Dungeons & Dragons discursively imagined and problematised by various Christian evangelical actors from 1979 to 1991?
To answer this rather large question, some sub-questions are also posed. They are as follows:
● How did the Christian evangelical actors imagine and problematise various aspects of Dungeons & Dragons? These include in-game religion, magic and occultism, the influence of the game on the mind of the player, and in-game moral issues.
● Who did the Christian evangelical actors imagine as the victim, and who did they imagine as the enemy? How were these two categories defined and problematised?
● How did the Christian evangelical actors relate themselves and Dungeons & Dragons to the state and other secular experts?
