TI  - Roll for Initiative: Balancing Player and Party in Library D&D Games
AU  - Nelson, Sarah Beth
AU  - and Matthews, Andra
T2  - Public Library Quarterly
AB  - Since its inception in the mid-’70s, Dungeons & Dragons (D&D) has been drawing players into collaborative quests. Increasingly, public libraries are hosting D&D games. Researchers observed D&D games hosted in two public libraries and interviewed participants, seeking to learn: How do D&D games in libraries foster individuality and community? Players pursue individual interests while also acting as considerate party members. Libraries provide D&D-related resources and participants find library games especially welcoming. The library enacts its role as a democratic institution by allowing a diverse group of people to play with a balance between individual and community. Other public libraries may be inspired to create their own D&D program or host similar events of interest to their staff and patrons.
