TI  - Generative Role-Playing Game-Based Learning in a Higher Education Literature Course
AU  - Cooper, Rich Paul
AU  - Chowdury, Mahjabin
AU  - Donaldson, Jonan Phillip
AU  - Barth, Madeleine
AB  - This study examines the effectiveness of generative role-playing game-based learning in a college-level science fiction course. Twenty-three students participated in a semester-long role-playing game designed to enhance understanding of literary concepts and foster internal motivation to learn. The study incorporated seven theoretical frameworks, including constructionist theory, situated learning theory, and transformative learning theory. Findings reveal that role-playing games provided transformational learning experiences, particularly in identity exploration and critical thinking. The study also suggests that roleplaying games can be leveraged as a powerful tool for transformational growth across various disciplines, encouraging educators to consider how identity exploration through role-play can enhance learning outcomes in different educational contexts.
