TI  - Folklore and Region in Actual Play Podcasting: An Interview with Clint and Griffin McElroy
AU  - Waugh-Quasebarth, Jasper
AU  - McElroy, Clint
AU  - McElroy, Griffin
T2  - Appalachian Journal
AB  - "Actual play" podcasts of players of tabletop roleplaying games (TTRPG) such as Dungeons & Dragons have emerged as a growing genre of story-telling. Listeners tune in to the hundreds of podcasts available online and subscribe to entire media channels dedicated to this content. Among the most popular is The Adventure Zone, an actual play podcast featuring the McElroy family: brothers Justin, Travis, Griffin, and father Clint.1 The show has taken place over several narrative arcs with the family changing roles of the Game Manager (GM) and Player Characters as the setting and style of TTRPG changes. Like many role-playing, narrative-driven games, their work draws upon folklore while engaging with themes like loss, power, memory—all balanced with a rich sense of humor. The Adventure Zone's second major narrative arc, Amnesty, for which Griffin served as the GM, followed monster hunters based in a fictionalized version of the mountains of eastern West Virginia. As a folklorist of West Virginian expressive and environmental traditions and a lover of nerd culture and role-playing games, I was drawn to the show for its treatment of the region and the people who live there. On January 23, 2024, I conducted an interview via videoconference with Griffin (Washington, D.C.) and Clint (Ironton, Ohio) to explore the intersections of folklore and place in their storytelling practices.
