TY  - JOUR
TI  - Developing and Testing an Explanatory Model of Complete Mental Health in Tabletop Role-Playing Games
AU  - McLaren, Patrick J
AU  - Slemp, Gavin R
AU  - Deery, Ben
AU  - Oades, Lindsay G
T2  - Psychology of Popular Media
AB  - There is growing interest in the therapeutic potential of tabletop role-playing games (TTRPGs), with practitioners and not-for-profits using TTRPGs to support social connection and social skill development. However, this interest has generated a practice-research gap, with practice vastly outpacing the research supporting it. Addressing this gap is vital to ensure the use of TTRPGs as an intervention is evidence-based. This is particularly true given the stigma faced by games like Dungeons and Dragons in the 1980s. The aim of this study is to address this gap by exploring the relationship between psychological need satisfaction, social connectedness, and social–emotional development and their relationship with TTRPGs and complete mental health among adult players. This study develops and tests an explanatory model of complete mental health in TTRPGs using self-determination theory and the complete state mental health model. In total, 766 casual TTRPG players participated in an online survey. The results of a structural equation model indicate that social connectedness and social–emotional development fully mediate the relationship between need satisfaction in TTRPGs and well-being. However, relationships with mental illness symptomology were mixed. While this study supports a relationship between TTRPGs and well-being, the relationship between TTRPG play and mental illness symptomology needs to be explored further.

Public Policy Relevance Statement

This study developed and tested a model of complete mental health in tabletop role-playing games (TTRPGs), which showed this game genre may meet basic psychological needs and promote social connectedness and social–emotional development in players, which may then improve mental health. This study provides preliminary theoretical and empirical evidence to inform ongoing research in the therapeutic application of TTRPGs.
