TY  - CONF
TI  - The Room Where It Happened: Reactionary Misogyny in TTRPG in Portugal
AU  - Cunha, Ana
AU  - Marques, Tiago Carvalheiro
T2  - KISMIF Conference 2024
AB  - Geek masculinity domains have been historically exclusionary of queer, racialized,
and feminine subjects. TTRPG are no exception. Built as safe spaces for marginalized
unconventional masculinities, they are rejecting newcomers of different demographics
that might threaten the established order. The aggrieved entitlement that male
subjects are socialized to experience, along with the increased visibility conquered by
feminized, racialized, and queer individuals, has sparked reactionary movements such
as GamerGate or The Fappening. Thus, we look at the case study of a small Portuguese
TTRPG publisher whose products promote hate speech under the guise of satire and
artistic freedom, known for controversial stances and self-victimization practices in
response to retaliation from platforms whose guidelines they purposefully breach.
Through a sociological and critical discourse analysis lens, we examine the content of
the game MEN, identifying stereotypes, rhetorical structures, shock-inducing strategies,
and underlying ideology. We also look at the discourse surrounding the product and
the company in subreddit forums, Discord groups, and official marketing on X, from
the perspective of Actor-Network Theory, assessing how the logistics of these platforms
either encourage or discourage this type of discourse. Finally, we evaluate how rage
baiting strategies and indirect hate speech fit into a historical trend of conservative
populist discourse. This paper combines linguistic, sociological, feminist, masculinist,
and queer sensibilities, in an intersectional perspective, essential for a comprehensive
analysis of this phenomenon.
