TY  - THES
TI  - Designing the UI of a Virtual Playing Board for Tabletop Roleplaying Games
AU  - Bruno, Ia
AB  - There exists a lot of digital tools for tabletop role-playing games (TTRPGs), but many of these digital tools require a lot of preparation from Game Masters. Because of this, being a Game Master can seem daunting, but having a digital tool to ease this workload could make it easier and more enjoyable to play the Game Master role. One such tool could be a virtual playing board with a virtual environment to mimic the typical environments that are encountered in TTRPGs. This thesis aimed to research the requirements of such a virtual playing board and its user interface, by identifying the needs of Game Masters and players of TTRPGs. This was done through a pre-study to identify the preliminary requirements and a low- and high-fidelity prototype stage where testing occurred in order to evaluate the user experience and validate the requirements. The features of the virtual playing board were designed to be useful and intuitive for both new and experienced Game Masters by using principles such as usability, recognition, clarity, and consistency. The immersion for players was also explored, as well as the enjoyment of the virtual playing board and how it compared to the usual methods of playing for both Game Masters and players. The research conducted revealed a vast and diverse set of expectations from both Game Masters and players regarding the functionality of a virtual playing board, but all features that were developed and tested in the high-fidelity prototype were deemed useful from the testing responses. Players generally found the tool to be an improvement over traditional methods, whilst some Game Masters expressed reluctance to switch from the methods they are currently using. In terms of immersion, environment sound was the most important aspect to players, but many of the other features also received high ratings.
