TY  - JOUR
TI  - Corporate Caesarism: From Utopia to Cyberpunk
AU  - Rajak, Nemanja
AB  - This essay, Corporate Caesarism: From Utopia to Cyberpunk, proposes a new concept — Corporate Caesarism — as the terminal phase of Western civilization. Drawing upon the cyclical philosophy of Oswald Spengler, the economic historiography of Fernand Braudel, and the cultural hauntology of Mark Fisher, the text traces the evolution of capitalism from its urban–mercantile origins to its postmodern corporate empire. Through a civilizational and philosophical lens, the essay interprets how democracy, once the instrument of money, dissolves into technocratic rule by global corporations. This form of Caesarism, unlike the personal despotisms of antiquity, is algorithmic, networked, and impersonal — a digital imperium where the corporation assumes the role of sovereign. The work moves from economic history and ideology critique to cultural prophecy, concluding that the West’s final ruler is no longer a man, but a machine with a human face.
