AB  - Dungeons and Dragons (D&D) has been considered to be an intellectually challenging game for strategy planning and role-playing. Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed as autonomous or semi-autonomous agents, yet most evaluations still target single-turn QA or short-horizon tasks. Assessing agentic performance in rules-constrained, multi-step settings is challenging because styleconforming narration can diverge from task optimality. In this work, we present D&D Agents, a multi-agent Dungeons & Dragons simulator. In our simulator, LLMs use tools to query and update the game state, assuming the roles of referee (’Dungeon Master’, DM), players, and adversarial monsters in tactically rich combat. Such simulation requires long-horizon planning, compliance with game ∗Equal contribution.
