TY  - CHAP
TI  - From Diverse Roots to Dual Classing: Crystallizing the Role of Tabletop Role-Playing Games in Education, with Examples from Corporate Learning and Development
AU  - Hartyándi, Mátyás
T2  - Education and Analog Role-Playing Games
A2  - Haarman, Susan
AB  - Contrary to popular belief, role-playing games (RPGs) are not merely recreational activities that began with the release of Dungeons & Dragons (D&D) in 1974. The phrase “role-playing game” has been used in human resource development since the 1950s, referring to psychodramatic contests and crisis-playing political simulations. Educators have long utilized various forms of role-playing as learning methods. The educational application of analog leisure RPGs and their related research has recently surged, especially in the last decade.
This study examines the educational potential of tabletop RPGs in adult education, taking corporate learning and development (L&D) interventions as examples because they are substantial settings for non-formal learning, often characterized by bold, creative, and well-financed initiatives. Although role-playing may appear too whimsical for the serious world of adulthood and business, it has been a vital part of business and management education since the 1940s, used for diagnosing, informing, and training. This rich context helps to crystallize the actual educational value of tabletop RPGs.
Using comparative analysis, the study distinguishes applied tabletop RPGs from other interactive interventions, such as business simulations, case studies, situational exercises, and various action methods utilizing complex role-playing. The study employs a three-layered framework – social, ludic, and diegetic – to analyze RPGs. This model highlights how tabletop RPGs blend complex role assumptions and game mechanics, setting them apart from other role-playing forms. The findings suggest that tabletop RPGs’ educational utility and added value may lie in their ability to facilitate cognitive engagement, simulate complex decision-making scenarios, and promote probabilistic thinking. Two approaches to “dual classing” are outlined regarding the interplay of complex role-playing with the ludic layers: one using referee-managed rules to prioritize flexibility and experimentation, and the other employing player-facing rules to teach real-world dynamics and reinforce cognitive and behavioral learning.
