TY  - CHAP
TI  - We Are Playing in a Material World: Thinking and Learning through the Physical Objects of Tabletop Role-Playing Games
AU  - Hautsch, Jessica
T2  - Education and Analog Role-Playing Games
A2  - Haarman, Susan
AB  - Often, when we discuss tabletop role-playing games, such as Dungeons & Dragons, we discuss mental escapism and imagination. These discursive frameworks often depict Dungeons & Dragons as intangible and immaterial, happening in what is sometimes referred to as the “theater of the mind.” This chapter challenges that understanding of the game, arguing that the physical objects of the game are not only a part of how we play but also how we think. Drawing on and building upon the work of scholars who have examined the material components of role-playing games, I argue that as we interact with rulebooks, character sheets, and dice, they are integrated into our cognitive system, lightening the mental load and facilitating play. Learning to play entails understanding how to use these environmental supports to extend our minds by distributing cognition across them.
