TY  - CHAP
TI  - Collaboration and Empowerment: Reimagining Literacy in the Composition Classroom through Tabletop Role-Playing Games
AU  - Brockway, David
AU  - Haarman, Susan
T2  - Education and Analog Role-Playing Games
AB  - This chapter reconsiders the definition of literacy through the lens of social constructivism, positioning it not as a fixed set of reading and writing skills but as a dynamic, socially situated practice shaped by dialogue, emotion, and context. Drawing on educational theorists such as Paulo Freire and Carol Lee and work from the field of games studies by James Paul Gee and Gary Alan Fine, this chapter argues for an expanded, inclusive definition of literacy that embraces multiple modes of meaning-making – especially those cultivated in tabletop role-playing games (tabletop RPGs). In both composition classrooms and tabletop RPG communities, participants learn to navigate complex social dynamics, construct shared narratives, and engage in collaborative, interpretive practices. These overlapping literacies foreground the importance of social and emotional learning (SEL), empowering students and players alike to take risks, develop agency, and participate more fully in knowledge-making. By bringing educational theory into conversation with game studies, this chapter proposes a reflective composition pedagogy that leverages the collaborative behaviors of tabletop RPGs to reimagine classroom dynamics and challenge traditional hierarchies in education.
