TY  - CHAP
TI  - Introduction to Education and Analog Role-Playing Games: Theory and Pedagogy
AU  - Haarman, Susan
AU  - Haarman, Susan
T2  - Education and Analog Role-Playing Games
AB  - This introduction grounds the volume Education and Analog Role-Playing Games: Theory and Pedagogy by situating analog role-playing games (RPGs) (including tabletop RPGs and live action role-play) as powerful, underexplored pedagogical tools. Arguing that these games cultivate empathy, identity exploration, and critical thinking, even outside of formal educational settings, this chapter outlines the need to move beyond proving that RPGs can educate to asking why they do so effectively. It frames analog RPGs as sites of experiential, affective, and relational learning – spaces where knowledge is co-created rather than transmitted. This chapter previews this book’s structure across three thematic sections: pedagogical foundations, disciplinary intersections, and inclusive educational practices. By centering co-creation, improvisation, and embodied engagement, this chapter calls for a reimagining of pedagogy through the lens of analog play and positions this volume as a collective intervention into educational theory and practice.
