TY  - CHAP
TI  - Historiography and History Role-Playing Games: The Past is What We Play It
AU  - Mallory, Stephen
T2  - Education and Analog Role-Playing Games
A2  - Haarman, Susan
AB  - This chapter defines history tabletop role-playing games (hTTRPGs), how game designers and players are complicit in creating alternative histories and ahistorical narratives that can reinforce and resist culturally warped popular histories. These games can encourage designers and players to engage/reengage with historical works while emphasizing an implicit requirement for textual analysis and criticality by illuminating popular history agendas, biases, and frameworks. In so doing, hTTRPG participants can inadvertently or explicitly repeat and amplify historical narratives of dubious research or assertion and highlight how these games walk blur the distinctions between historical recreation, science fiction alternate histories, and historiography. By blurring these areas, game designers, participants, and scholars are placed at the forefront of creating and instilling greater material and ethical standards for games that portray the past and prevent the silencing of the horrors of the past in the name of good gameplay.
