TY  - CHAP
TI  - Transforming Crisis into Growth: Neurodivergence, Bleed, and Care Ethics in RPGs
AU  - Spencer, Albert R.
T2  - Education and Analog Role-Playing Games: TeachRPG – Tabletop Role-Playing Games in the Classroom, Volume 1
A2  - Haarman, Susan
AB  - This chapter explores the transformative potential of role-playing games (RPGs) as tools for personal growth and identity exploration, with a focus on neurodivergent players who may face heightened emotional intensity, rejection sensitivity, and unresolved trauma. Using the author’s case study of playing a Warforged Cleric named Reaper, this chapter examines how emotional bleed – when in-game and out-of-game experiences overlap – can lead to crises that traditional consent-based safety tools are ill-equipped to address. It argues that while informed consent is necessary for safe play, it is insufficient, particularly for neurodivergent players, unless it is grounded within care ethics, which prioritize empathy, communication, and collaborative problem-solving. This chapter first surveys the current literature on therapeutic RPGs, identifying their unique capacity to foster self-awareness, emotional resilience, and social connection. It then analyzes the Reaper case study to illustrate how moments of crisis can be transformed into opportunities for growth through relational processing, narrative scaffolding, and structured debriefing. By integrating insights from care ethics and neurodivergence research, this chapter demonstrates how intentional care practices can enhance the inclusivity and educative potential of RPGs. Ultimately, it presents a roadmap for facilitators to create supportive environments that transform crises into meaningful connections and foster personal and communal growth.
