TY  - CHAP
TI  - Storyworlding Together: Tabletop Role-Playing Games as Inclusive Becoming
AU  - Gustafson, Kari
T2  - Education and Analog Role-Playing Games: TeachRPG – Tabletop Role-Playing Games in the Classroom, Volume 1
A2  - Haarman, Susan
AB  - This chapter considers role-playing games as a collaborative storytelling practice and explores how a diffractive reading of gameplay and game mechanics, critical disability and neurodiversity scholarship, and educational theory can provide insights into shaping inclusive learning spaces. Through key moments in a tabletop role-playing game experience, this chapter illustrates the concept of storyworlding, where characters, identities, and story emerge at once through practices of relationality and reciprocity. Storyworlding processes are supported by game mechanics that promote player and character diversity, challenge linear and predetermined trajectories of development, and require radical power-sharing between all participants, including the gamemaster. As an educator, playing and thinking with role-playing games can challenge classroom systems and practices that center “typical” development, and instead suggest and model inclusive community building, and learning as a process of becoming otherwise together.
