TY  - JOUR
TI  - Tabletop Role-Playing Games and the History Classroom
AU  - Guerty, Phillip
T2  - The Journal of Cinema and Media Studies
AB  - Role-playing games (RPGs) are powerful educational tools, revolutionizing how students engage with complex subjects. Since the 1990s, instructors have used both computer-based games and analog role-playing exercises—such as tabletop RPGs and live-action scenarios—to supplement traditional lectures and engage students more meaningfully, especially in the teaching of history. This essay will examine how RPGs are currently used in college history classes, focusing specifically on tabletop role-playing games (TTRPGs). A distinctive feature of TTRPGs is their function as pedagogical tools and primary sources. As primary sources, they offer a window into the past that allows students to better understand the social, cultural, and political dimensions of the time in which they were created. As pedagogical tools, they enable students to better comprehend the place of context, empathy, and contingency in history.
