TY  - CHAP
TI  - Domain Knowledge in Tabletop Roleplaying Games
AU  - Heron, Michael
T2  - Demystifying Game Studies
AB  - Along with the more abstract analytical perspectives we discuss regarding game studies, this book also seeks to provide some broader context on the three primary forms of medium that are the focus. In this chapter, we look at Tabletop Roleplaying Games, or TTRPGs, as an intensely social gaming style that often deeply challenges the fundamental assumptions made about the magic circle. We begin with some history, explaining where the game format comes from – tracing it all the way from chess through to Prussian wargames and through to Dungeons and Dragons. We look at the common tropes and expectations, and how other popular (and sometimes obscure) gaming systems uphold, subvert or reject these expectations. We also introduce some analytical tools that readers may find useful – the LARP mixing desk; the Game/Narrative/Simulation model, and several conceptualizations of what rules mean within a TTRPG.
