TI  - Collaborative Role-Playing Integrative Therapy (CRIT): A Theoretical Bridge Between Tabletop Role-Playing and Group Therapy Through Dialectical Therapeutic Factors
AU  - Dehili, Vincent M.
AU  - Merrill, Brett M.
AU  - Chun, Sami M.
AU  - Merten, E.
AU  - King, Matt
T2  - Group
AB  - This article is the second in a planned, three-part series that
explores the utility of tabletop role-playing games (TTRPGs) in
therapy. It introduces collaborative role-playing integrative
therapy (CRIT), a transtheoretical framework for utilizing TTRPGs
in group therapy. Much like Yalom's common factors approach, this
model identifies nine dialectical therapeutic factors that
illustrate how TTRPGs foster interpersonal growth and psychological
flexibility. These nine dialectical factors are self and system,
intent and impact, play and work, reality and imagination,
vulnerability and safety, ambition and equanimity, spontaneity and
structure, generality and specificity, and here and now and there
and then. As a novel therapeutic model, CRIT offers a rich
landscape for future research and application, expanding the
potential of TTRPGs as a transformative group therapy modality.
