TI  - Bodies and Time in Tabletop Role-Playing Game Combat System
AU  - Torner, Evan
T2  - The Wyrd Con Companion Book 2015
A2  - Bowman, Sarah Lynne
AB  - This article examines combat systems across many tabletop role-playing games (TRPGs) as discursive objects sharing a common thread: they follow the model of Dungeons & Dragons by creating an experience of “special time” in which the object of the game suddenly becomes the elimination of the opponents’ bodies, which are quantified as hit points. Using a cultural studies framework, the piece contends that this core combat experience is fundamentally ideological, ingrained in the TRPG medium, and delivers a specific affect around the variable waiting for the bodies of others to disappear so that other types of play can continue. In this respect, the paper demonstrates how we might look at how TRPGs manage player time, represent bodies, manage affect, and reward specific types of meta-play.
