TI  - Participatory Storytelling as a Media Platform: TRPGs, Gamebooks, Readers' Columns, and Play-by-Mail in Japanese Analog RPGs
AU  - Saito, Satomi
T2  - Handbook of Japanese Games and Gameplay
AB  - Tabletop role-playing games (TRPGs) were introduced to Japan in the 1980s by Yasuda Hitoshi and his company, Group SNE, through his early efforts in localizing American TRPGs and translating the single-player role-playing gamebooks written by Steve Jackson. By becoming a platform that allowed users to tell stories and participate in world-building collaboratively, the Japanese TRPG soon played a significant role in the success of Japan's media mix business led by the publisher Kadokawa Shoten. This chapter examines the unique development of Japanese TRPGs in the 1990s, discussing various applications of the TRPG platform, such as TRPG replays, the readers’ column, and play-by-mail TRPGs.
