TI  - Roll for Discernment Dungeon Master as St. Ignatius’s Spiritual Director
AU  - Haarman, Susan
T2  - Theology, Religion, and Dungeons & Dragons: Explorations of the Sacred through Fantasy Worlds
A2  - Donahue-Martens, Scott
A2  - Simonson, Brandon
AB  - Role-playing games like Dungeons & Dragons (D&D) provide opportunities for formative experiences for players. The game’s role-play demands a level of imagination, participatory commitment, self-reflection, creative problem-solving, and collaboration from players that most leisure activities do not. The bulk of scholarship around tabletop role-playing games’ (TTRPGs’) impact focuses primarily on players and often glosses over the essential role of the individual facilitating the gaming experience. The game master (called a Dungeon Master in D&D, hereafter referred to as a DM) is an individual who is both the facilitator and participant in the game. DMs are charged with creating the details and challenges of adventures while maintaining a realistic continuity of events. They have the power to control any element in the game other than the most potent one: the characters’ choices. While style of play and level of influence can vary from game to game, DMs are as impacted by their players’ decisions as players are by their DMs’ decisions....
