TY  - THES
TI  - Storytelling in the Digital Age
AU  - Merriman, Tobias
AB  - Storytelling in the Digital Age examines the evolution of narrative art through tabletop role-playing games (TTRPGs), emphasizing how the TTRPG as a medium coupled with modern technologies foster creativity, accessibility, and narrative immersion. TTRPGs offer profound storytelling potential analogous to literature, theater, and cinema. The core of this medium is connection—between storyteller and audience, game master (GM) and players, and among players themselves—creating a dynamic, collaborative narrative experience. Unlike traditional storytelling media, TTRPGs necessitate co-authorship between players and the GM. Players maintain autonomy over their characters' choices and histories, collaboratively woven into a non-deterministic yet coherent narrative. The GM's role extends beyond referee; they function as primary author, narrator, director, and world-builder. This role requires comprehensive mastery of the game world's details, ensuring internal consistency and immersion while preserving player agency. This thesis demonstrates how digital tools and platforms can expand the traditional boundaries of storytelling, enabling richer narrative experiences, fostering greater accessibility, and creating communities that transcend physical limitations. Both these technologies and the concepts of experiential learning through co-writing have greater implications beyond their origination with respect to remote work and distance learning outcomes. This thesis positions TTRPGs not merely as games, but as vibrant, dynamic, and significant cultural and narrative forms of the digital age. AKA “MFA in D&D.”
