TY  - THES
TI  - The Power of Play: Tabletop Roleplaying Games as a Psychosocial Intervention for Structurally Marginalized Youth
AU  - Rorem Colquhoun, Devyn C
AB  - Tabletop role-playing games (TTRPGs) are experiencing a surge in popularity. Once a stigmatized pastime, TTRPGs have become inclusive community spaces for structurally marginalized youth. Alongside this cultural shift is growing recognition of TTRPGs not only as supports for healthy psychological development but as promising vehicles for psychosocial intervention. Responding to a community-identified need, we investigate TTRPGs as mechanisms for youth development and intervention. We analyzed how Level Up Gaming League Edmonton implements TTRPGs with structurally marginalized youth with the research aim to amplify participants’ perspectives on the services designed for them. Using a community-engaged, qualitative descriptive approach, we interviewed youth (N = 10), Dungeon Masters (N = 5), and community partners at host sites (N = 2). Findings highlight impacts across key developmental domains, including social, emotional, identity, and moral development, and identify program components that youth and Dungeon Masters view as essential to those impacts. Results underscore the importance of strength-based approaches that leverage youths’ interests and provide a healthy environment for learning and growth. Recommendations for clinicians and service providers are provided. This work contributes to the emerging TTRPG literature by sharing insights from a Canadian community-based program designed for structurally marginalized youth and to the youth-intervention literature by illustrating how innovative approaches can harness known resilience-promoting processes.
