TY  - THES
TI  - Below the Depths: How Tabletop Role-Playing Games Facilitate the Individuation Process
AU  - Sykes, R. Chad
AB  - Research suggests that individual psychotherapy can support the client’s individuation process. However, many potential clients hesitate to engage within the traditional context that therapeutic services are often delivered. Fantasy-based tabletop role-playing games (TTRPGs) may serve as an alternative container for unfolding the individuation process, given its significant overlap with the structure of group therapy, interventions of narrative therapy, and symbolism of depth psychology. This hermeneutic and artistic–creative study examines the current literature on TTRPGs as a therapeutic resource and proposes a 6-week workshop demonstrating how clinicians could implement TTRPGs in a group therapy setting. Findings suggest that TTRPGs positively support the individuation process through the externalization of issues, group cocreation of myth, and experiential engagement with the hero’s journey. Recommendations are provided for further research of concepts explored in this study pertaining to the applicability of the TTRPG workshop model for a variety of populations.
