TY  - VIDEO
TI  - Playing as Inquiry: A Multimodal Approach to Transformative Tabletop RPGs
AB  - Full title: Playing as Inquiry: A Multimodal Approach to Transformative Tabletop Role-Playing Games

This lecture was originally recorded Jan 13, 2026.

Description:
Tabletop role-playing games (TTRPGs) can function as cultural laboratories for experiential learning, identity work, and conflict negotiation, yet most studies still approach them from the outside, using surveys or post hoc interviews. In this seminar, Cristo Leon presents Playing as Inquiry: a multimodal method that treats actual play as both the site of transformation and the primary source of data. Building on collaborative autoethnography and constructivist grounded theory, the León Multimodal Protocol (LMP) combines detailed audio and video transcription with layered coding of speech, rule interactions, gesture, spatial positioning, and symbolic references at the table.
This lecture will discuss findings from a five-hour video recording of a play session, its full transcript, and other materials. The session was played using Leon's Cosmic System, a meta role-playing game system currently in beta testing. Leon will discuss how the use of the LMP invites participants to revisit the session as co-analysts, turning transcription into a reflective tool that supports metacognitive awareness of how transformative moments are constructed in interaction.
Bio:
Cristo Leon, Ph.D., is the Director of Research for the Jordan Hu College of Science and Liberal Arts at the New Jersey Institute of Technology (NJIT) and Editor-in-Chief of the Journal of Role-Playing Studies and STEAM (JRPSSTEAM). His work focuses on empowering collaborative co-creation in research through storytelling, transdisciplinary communication, and human-centered design—making projects fundable, meaningful, and impactful. As a researcher and educator, he advances diversity, equity, and inclusion (DEI) while developing open educational resources (OER), Critical Virtual Exchange (CVE) practices, and experiential learning models that use role-playing pedagogies to foster global, interdisciplinary collaboration.
* * *
This series is hosted by the Games & Society Lab at the Department of Game Design, Uppsala University Campus Gotland. The series explores the use of analog role-playing games as vehicles for lasting personal and social change.
