TY  - CONF
TI  - Empathy in Action: Leveraging Role-Playing Games (RPGs) as Suicide Prevention and Identification Tools
AU  - Lam, Huy
AB  - This study aims to explore the potential of role-playing games (RPGs) as effective tools for suicide prevention and the early identification of mental health challenges among youth. RPGs provide immersive experiences that foster empathy, enhance communication, and create supportive environments for emotional expression. We hypothesize that these characteristics facilitate the identification of at-risk individuals and improve mental well-being. A mixed-methods approach is employed, including a systematic review of existing literature on RPGs and mental health, qualitative interviews and surveys of participants engaged in RPG sessions. A cohort of 100 participants, aged 18–25, is selected to investigate the impact of RPGs on emotional openness, empathy development, and mental health awareness. RPG facilitators also receive specialized training to identify signs of suicidal ideation and emotional distress during gameplay. Pre- and post-session assessments are used to measure changes in empathy levels, communication abilities, and emotional resilience, while qualitative data from interviews and facilitator observations provide further insights into emotional expression and mental health awareness. Preliminary findings indicate that RPGs create an environment that encourages participants to safely explore emotions and develop empathy, resulting in a greater sense of belonging and social connection. Participants report increased empathy, improved communication skills, and enhanced emotional resilience. Facilitators successfully identify warning signs of emotional distress, leading to early interventions for several participants. These findings suggest that RPGs hold promise as innovative, complementary tools for suicide prevention and early intervention, particularly among youth, by fostering empathy, promoting emotional communication, and supporting mental health awareness.
