TY  - CHAP
TI  - A Genealogy of Holy Weapons: Dungeons & Dragons and the Weaponification of Religion in (Video)games
AU  - Wildt, Lars de
AU  - Heede, Pieter Van den
T2  - The Meaning of Weapons
A2  - Liere, Lucien van
A2  - Meinema, Erik
AB  - In this chapter, we trace a genealogy of holy weapons in video games. We identify Dungeons & Dragons as the root of a ‘weaponification’ of religion: a specific development of the meaning of weapons in games. To study this development, we draw on the pivotal case study of the role-playing game Dungeons & Dragons, which was central to the transition from wargaming to role-playing games and remains a source for game design conventions that have become widely adopted since. Through Dungeons & Dragons's weapons, we genealogically trace four historical processes: (1) the quantification of weaponry as a key system within wargaming; (2) the fictionalization of said wargame weaponry during the 1970s through the gradual development of tabletop role-playing games such as Dungeons & Dragons; (3) the encyclopaedicization of the latter through the proliferation of rulebooks; and (4) the culmination of these prior steps into what we identify as the ‘weaponification of religion’ in both analogue and video games. This results in four ideal-typical holy weapons: divine ‘modifiers’ added to classical weapons, the use of religious artefacts as weapons, words as weapons, and Gods as weapons. We will emphasize their relevance for the meaning of weapons beyond the study of video games and the study of religion.
