TY  - JOUR
TI  - “The Trial of Silver Tongues”: Gamifying persuasion through Dungeons & Dragons
AU  - Lamura, Gabrielle
T2  - Communication Teacher
AB  - Persuasion extends beyond the page, whether through a movie, book, or even a tabletop game. In this teaching activity designed for COM 4712: Writing to Persuade, I developed a tabletop role-playing game using a modified Dungeons & Dragons framework to immerse students in persuasive communication through experiential role-play. Guided by instructor as Dungeon Master, students participate in a one shot featuring quests that require them to persuade, negotiate, reason, and collaborate within an evolving narrative in which words (and occasionally weapons) determine success. Grounded in Aristotelian rhetoric (Aristotle, 2007) and experiential learning theory (Kolb, 1984), this activity transforms abstract rhetorical concepts into active social experiences. Structured in three stages, the assignment fosters collaboration, critical thinking, and ethical reflection. This approach demonstrates the pedagogical value of narrative-based learning and gamified instruction in communication courses. By situating persuasion within interactive storytelling, students experience rhetoric as an evolving negotiation of credibility, emotion, and logic, which in turn prepares them to apply persuasive principles across academic, professional, and civic contexts. After completing this activity, students will be able to: (1) identify and apply the rhetorical appeals of ethos, pathos, and logos in live persuasive contexts. (2) Use persuasive language strategically to advance group objectives. (3) Reflect on the ethical and emotional dimensions of persuasion and audience adaptation; and (4) Connect their experiential performance to rhetorical theory in subsequent analysis and writing.
