TI  - Playing with Autism: Using Play in Psychoanalytic Work with Adult Autistics
AU  - Fink, Jennifer Natalya
T2  - Psychoanalytic Inquiry
AB  - Play is the cornerstone of therapy with children. As Anna Freud noted, “Children learn best through play and exploration.” Yet it is often assumed that play is inaccessible to autistic people: that they literally don’t know how to play. A failure to play—or rather to perform behaviors that are easily recognized as play by neurotypical people—is even used as a diagnostic criterion for neurodivergent disabilities. Yet autistic kids—and adults—do play. Recognizing and honoring autistic play, knowing how to join in the play, and using autistic play analytically/therapeutically without extinguishing or changing its autistic qualities are key to working with autistic people of all ages. This article explores some key facets of using play in analytic work with autistic adults—both those that are similar to allists (non-autistics) and those that are distinctive to the neuropsychology and culture of autism. Using 2 case studies, I examine scripting and role-playing games as distinctively autistic forms of play that are rich sources for analytic work. Instead of changing how autistic adults play, what happens when allists join autists, playing on their terms? How might psychoanalysis be enriched by neurodiverse play?
