TY  - JOUR
TI  - Serious games based on scripted mystery role-playing: Exploring tensions between legal and social ethical awareness through game-based pedagogy
AU  - Xie, Ziyi
AU  - Duan, Zhizhuang
T2  - Learning and Instruction
AB  - Background
Legal awareness and social ethical awareness are both unified and contested; how individuals respond to the tension between the two in specific contexts is a key area of concern in civic education. However, evidence derived from the use of game-based pedagogy to examine students concrete responses to this relationship remains scarce.
Aims
This study investigates the specific manifestations of and response strategies to tensions between legal awareness and social ethical awareness among non-law major university students, thereby providing practical implications for the further application of game-based pedagogy in civic education.
Sample
A total of 48 undergraduate students without a law major participated in the serious game designed for this study.
Methods
Grounded in the renowned legal case The Case of the Speluncean Explorers, this study developed a serious game based on game-based pedagogy and employed a qualitative research design using thematic analysis to code and examine students in-game dialogues and post-game reflective statements.
Results
The findings revealed that students exhibited conflicting awareness across three dimensions: social structure and interpersonal equality, social contribution and individual value, and interest-based contracts and fairness and justice. In most cases, social ethical awareness considerations tended to take precedence over legal awareness.
Conclusions
The findings can provide useful references for further promoting the concrete application of game-based pedagogy in educational settings and for improving current civic education practices.
