TI  - Fantasy Meets Function: Dungeons & Dragons as a Tool for Social and Emotional Learning
AU  - Stevens, Jeremiah
AB  - This project sought to understand if using the popular tabletop roleplaying game, Dungeons & Dragons, as an afterschool program would improve preadolescents social and emotional learning. Therefore, a 10-week D&D program was created and implemented, using the CASEL framework as guidance. Approximately 3-5 participants engaged with the program for one-hour sessions. Each session included a self-contained adventure with scenarios that were specifically designed to increase SEL. D&D is low-cost and requires minimal equipment and space, making this program adaptable and easily fit anywhere into club program hours. This project provided opportunities for participants to collaborate, build frustration tolerance, demonstrate leadership skills, develop empathy, and provide naturalistic learning opportunities.
