TY  - CHAP
TI  - Teaching Rolls: The Practice of Teaching and Game Mastering Through the Lens of John Dewey
AU  - Haarman, Susan
T2  - Education and Analog Role-Playing Games: TeachRPG – Tabletop Role-Playing Games in the Classroom, Volume 1
AB  - This chapter explores the striking parallels between the role of the game master (GM) in tabletop role-playing games and educational philosopher John Dewey’s vision of a teacher as articulated in Experience and Education. Drawing on the shared dynamics of authority, co-creation, and responsive facilitation, both GMs and teachers serve as leaders within collaborative communities, shaping experiences that are educative rather than directive. By placing Dewey’s educational philosophy in dialogue with practices of game mastering, this chapter identifies three overlapping best practices: co-creating meaningful experiences, establishing the norms of a creative community, and providing scaled, growth-oriented challenges. Ultimately, the piece offers a theory-building intervention that invites educators and game scholars alike to consider the mutual insights each field offers the other, particularly in how learning and play, grounded in experience, cultivate transformation, trust, and lifelong curiosity.
