AB  - In 
Chapter 13
, Thomas Apperley and Souvik Mukherjee argue that both board games and tabletop games are crucial for the study of culture(s) and their chapter explores the relationship between nondigital games and culture, framing nondigital games as pivotal cultural artefacts. The chapter follows the evolution of board games from historical epochs to contemporary times, and the more recent development of tabletop role-playing games, highlighting their importance in pedagogy and politics, and their potential for cultural expression and critique. As key issues here, archival research sheds light on the historical contexts of games, performance studies provide insights into the embodied practices of play, and decolonial approaches challenge Eurocentric narratives embedded in many games and gaming traditions.
