TY  - THES
TI  - Digital Table Top Role-Playing: A User Experience Perspective
AU  - Selleby, Siri
AU  - Bohman, Elin
AB  - Introduction

This thesis explores how current digital tools for Tabletop Role-Playing Games fulfill players needs, by looking at general features across systems and tools, as well as a case study of one of the most popular tools: D&D Beyond.

Research Question

How do players experience digital tools in tabletop role-playing games, and to what extent do these tools meet players’ needs?

Method

A mixed methods approach was used, featuring a survey with 46 participants and a case study. The survey examined commonly used tools, features and usability based on the System Usability Scale, content analysis was performed on the qualitative data. The case study encompassed a heuristic evaluation as well as user tests with ten individual participants.

Results

While player sentiment towards existing TTRPG tools were overall positive, many usability issues were found during the heuristic evaluation and validated by the user tests. These issues often impacted the user experience negatively. Additionally, the poor design appeared to frequently invoke negative feelings in users. Participants often utilized multiple digital tools simultaneously to cover all their needs. Furthermore, in terms of usability all three of the most popular TTRPG tools scored below average.

Discussion

The study resulted in four main findings. The genre of digital TTRPG tools might have a widespread issue of insufficient usability. Additionally, negative emotions caused by poor design might impact beneficial effects seen in previous research. Despite this, results showed a contrasting positive sentiment among players using the tools. Players were also found to often rely on supplementing the shortcomings of one tool with another. Finally, suggestions for how these findings can be further explored in future research are made.
