TY  - JOUR
TI  - Supporting Youth Literacy Practices through Three Models of Roleplaying Game Facilitation Structures
AU  - Jones, Karis
AU  - Corbitt, Alex
AU  - Lange-Fazio, Robin
AU  - Kohn, Alex
AU  - Lamb, Rob
T2  - Journal of Language & Literacy Education
AB  - Tabletop roleplaying games are compelling for literacy education as such games are often narrative-focused and can be adapted to support many learning objectives. This paper shares insights from voices in the field serving teens and adult learners across contexts (formal classrooms and library programming), with narrative vignettes exploring ways these educators adapted roleplaying games to meet their literacy learning goals in different ways. This paper explores three types of facilitation structures for tabletop roleplaying games: educator-led, youth-led, and decentralized. Each type of facilitation structure prioritizes different types of literacy learning practices, from personalized character development to collaborative problem solving to worldbuilding agency. We intend for these profiles to help educators consider their current contexts and what implementations of roleplaying games might be most useful to take up to serve their students.
