TY  - THES
TI  - Dungeons & Dragons as a Tool for Improving English Proficiency
AU  - Kačenová, Veronika
AB  - This thesis explores the impact of the tabletop role-playing game Dungeons and Dragons on
the communicative abilities of intermediate English language learners. With roots in the
principles of Communicative Language Teaching, the research investigates how game
mechanics, such as the character shield, provide a safe environment for language
experimentation and risk-taking.
The study used a qualitative methodology, tracking four adult participants through three
Dungeons and Dragons online sessions. Data collection included pre-sessions and post-sessions
questionnaires and audio recordings to analyse how individual language goals, ranging from
improving descriptive skills to increasing oral fluency, influenced linguistic output during
gameplay.
The findings demonstrate that D&D serves as an effective pedagogical facilitator. Participants
reported a significant increase in speaking confidence and successfully transitioned passive
vocabulary into active use due to the situational demands of the narrative. The research
concludes that the immersive, task-based nature of D&D promotes autonomous learning and
helps students overcome the anxiety typically associated with traditional, teacher-centered
classroom environments.
