TY  - THES
TI  - Practicing Empathy in The Assembly : A Mixed-Methods Study of Deliberative Larp
AU  - Gardner, Meghan
AU  - Tismanariu, Iléana
AU  - Ciubotaru, Alexandra
AB  - This thesis examines how deliberative live action role-playing games (larp) can support the practice of empathy and influence the participants’ capacity for deliberation, in the context of contemporary democratic challenges. It focuses on the study of a run of the deliberative larp entitled The Assembly (Bowman et al., 2025) and adopts a mixed-methods approach combining quantitative, qualitative, and observational data. Findings suggest that empathy in The Assembly operates across three interconnected levels: from player to their character, from character to character and from player to other characters, exposing players to the situated nature of the positions and increasing their awareness thereof. Rather than producing immediate opinion change, this empathic engagement fostered reflective political thinking and a broader awareness of deliberation as a relational and affective practice. The study further examines the specific design features enabling The Assembly to cultivate “empathy for the other side” and political reflection (Muradova, 2025) and identifies key features leveraging mechanics from both role-playing and deliberative practices. Overall, the study points to the potential of deliberative larp as a hybrid format that cultivates openness, empathy, and reflection, while highlighting the importance of design choices and the need for further research with more diverse participants.
