TY  - THES
TI  - Forking Paths and Living Worlds: Towards an immersive Immersive theatre
AU  - Tse, Cassandra
AB  - ‘Immersive theatre’ is a form of live performance that positions the audience within the aesthetic and narrative world of the event. ‘Immersion’ is a particular experiential quality — the feeling of losing oneself in a narrative or storyworld, of intensely engaging with a story and empathising with its characters, or of feeling like you have ‘become’ a character you are playing. I identify three layers of theatrical immersion: storyworld immersion, character immersion and narrative immersion, which can be combined to create ‘whole theatrical immersion’.In this thesis, I argue that Immersive theatre often struggles to achieve whole theatrical immersion, despite the promise inherent in its name. My project uses practice-based research, theory from video games, new media and live action role-play (larp), as well as existing theatre scholarship to determine whether a truly immersive Immersive theatre is possible at all.I put theory into practice by writing two texts for Immersive performance: Before We Slip Beneath the Sea and Farrago Bazaar. I collate and analyse the audience responses to these two plays and reflect on my practice-based research to determine whether immersion was achieved. Ultimately, I conclude that immersive Immersive theatre is possible when narrative is prioritised, the storyworld feels complete and audience self-awareness is eliminated. I also advocate for narrative designers to be included in Immersive practice.
