TY  - THES
TI  - The effects of a five-week Dungeons & Dragons campaign on mental well-being and basic psychological need satisfaction in university students : a pilot study.
AU  - Lin, Ying
AB  - This pilot study investigated whether a five-week Dungeons & Dragons (D&D) campaign increase in mental well-being of university students, and whether these improvement in mental well-being in relation to Self-Determination Theory. A pre-and-post-test design was employed with a sample of eight students. Mental well-being was measured using the Warwick-Edinburgh Mental Well-being Scale (WEMWBS), satisfaction of need (autonomy, competence, and relatedness) was assessed via the Basic Psychological Needs Scale (BPNS). Baseline autistic traits were quantified using the Autism-Spectrum Quotient (AQ-10), and loneliness was measured with the UCLA Loneliness Scale. Statistical analysis shows that after the D&D sessions, autonomy had an increase, with the t-test reaching significance (p = .049) and the Wilcoxon test approaching it closely (p = .053). Competence and relatedness moved in the expected direction but did not reach significance under either test. There were six out of eight participants exceeded the AQ-10 clinical threshold, thus the hypothesis that participants that have autistic traits will benefit more from D&D could not be evaluated. In addition, all eight participants finished all sessions, the retention is 100%, suggest that it is a good medium for intervention in the target population. The limitation of this study is small sample size (N = 8), A larger trial with sufficient statistical power may be better positioned to capture the effects that this study could only identify as directional trends.
