TY  - CONF
TI  - When the Story Stalls: Designing Narrative Scaffolds for Eudemonic Reflection
AU  - Stratton, Rowan
AU  - Sandbulte, Jomara
A2  - Fang, Xiaowen
AB  - Games can offer a space for self-exploration, where meaning emerges through players’ interpretation of identifiable themes. Experiences of stress similarly depend on how individuals interpret contextual events and draw on prior coping resources. Many serious games aim to support emotional resilience through scenario-based play, yet a gap often remains between narrative presentation and how players meaningfully interpret stress. In this work, we examine this gap by investigating how narrative play artifacts, used as playful research probes, support meaning-making around stress. We conducted a qualitative study with ($$N=30$$N=30) college students using an adapted tabletop role-playing game grounded in common student stressors. Using reflexive thematic analysis of collaboratively constructed narratives and post-play reflections, we examined how emotionally complex situations were interpreted through play. Our findings indicate that emotionally complex narrative play did not inherently support reflection when narrative meaning was underspecified, often leading to stalled interpretation. Introducing lightweight narrative scaffolding supported clearer sense-making while preserving player agency. These findings contribute design-oriented insights for serious games by positioning narrative scaffolding as an interpretive resource for meaningful engagement with stress among college students.
