TY  - THES
TI  - A Description of Conflicts Faced by Edgin Darvis in the Movie Dungeons & Dragons: Honor Among Thieves
AU  - Panjaitan, Jelita Gloria Aster
AB  - Background: Conflict is an important element in movies. Every conflict in a story usually leads to a resolution.  
Objective: This study aims to describe the types of conflict and their resolutions that Edgin Darvis faces in the movie Dungeons & Dragons: Honor Among Thieves.
Method: This study uses a descriptive qualitative method. The data were collected through dialogues, scenes, and actions presented in the movie. This study uses Kenny’s (1966) conflict theory and descriptive methods for the resolution of conflict at the end of the movie.
Results: This study explains that both types of conflict are found in the movie Dungeons & Dragons: Honor Among Thieves. Five internal conflicts are depicted through Edgin's fight with his thoughts, feelings, and emotions. Meanwhile, five external conflicts are evident in Edgin’s interactions with other characters. Regarding conflict resolution, two major resolutions have been identified, such as forgiving each other and accepting the loss.
Conclusion: By describing and examining types of conflicts and their resolutions, the audience can understand how conflict contributes to character development and influences the progression of the story.
