TI  - Creating Social Meaning Through Sound Symbolism and Aesthetics in Constructed Languages
AU  - Beinhoff, Bettina
T2  - The Palgrave Handbook of Constructed Languages
A2  - Windsor, Joseph W.
A2  - Long, Alison
AB  - This chapter investigates how social meaning is created in constructed languages, focusing on the role of sound symbolism and creator aesthetics. Drawing on interviews with language creators and naïve listeners, it identifies three main influences on language design: natural languages, fictional context and speakers, and aesthetic preferences. While some strategies, such as mellifluous syllables or harsh ejectives, elicited expected responses, others were interpreted inconsistently. The findings suggest that while sound symbolism can transcend linguistic boundaries, shared first languages do not guarantee shared perceptions. Ultimately, the study highlights the complex, intentional nature of language construction and the importance of context in shaping listener interpretation, advocating for broader cross-cultural research into language perception.
