TY  - JOUR
TI  - Construct operationalisation and outcome measurement in therapeutic tabletop role-playing game research: a systematic scoping review
AU  - Kilford, Emma
AU  - Hancox, Laura
AU  - Moghaddam, Nima
AB  - Tabletop role-playing games (TTRPGs) are increasingly being used intentionally for therapeutic and psychosocial purposes, but the emerging evidence base remains conceptually and methodologically heterogeneous. Existing reviews have mapped applications, reported outcomes and potential benefits, but have not focused specifically on how therapeutic outcomes and change-related constructs are defined, operationalised and assessed.

The objectives of this systematic scoping review are: (1) to describe how eligible studies conceptualise the therapeutic use of TTRPGs; (2) to identify the therapeutic outcomes and change-related constructs investigated in these studies and examine how they are defined and operationalised; (3) to chart the measures and assessment approaches used and examine their alignment with the identified constructs; and (4) to examine how the assessment approaches and study designs inform the interpretations and conclusions reported, and to identify resulting methodological and measurement priorities for future research.
