TY  - THES
TI  - Once upon a table: A qualitative examination of the effects of tabletop role-playing games on gender and sexual identity development and romantic relationships among members of the LGBTQ2S+ community
AU  - Breen, Lorna
AB  - This dissertation examined the psychosocial contributions of tabletop role-playing games (TRPGs) as sites for identity exploration, personal growth, relationship building, and community and political engagement among members of the Lesbian, Gay, Bisexual, Transgender, Queer, Two-Spirit, Plus (LGBTQ2S+) community across two exploratory qualitative studies. Drawing on complementary theoretical perspectives from positive LGBTQ2S+ identity research and critical queer and feminist theory, I centered the strengths and resources that LGBTQ2S+ individuals cultivate through the navigation of their identities and relationships, and positioned TRPGs as a potentially significant but largely unexamined context in which these processes might unfold. Across both studies, I employed reflexive thematic analysis to generate themes from my datasets. In Study 1, I utilized a digital ethnographic approach to generate and analyze a dataset of anonymous online public posts (N = 211) across four social media platforms authored by self-identified LGBTQ2S+ youth, young adults, and adults (N = 199). My analysis generated five overarching themes pertaining to the role of TRPGs in gender and sexual identity development: (1) Player/Character: Dynamic Identity Expression, Exploration, and Experimentation Through Role-Play, (2) In the Closet and at the Table: Navigating Safety and Visibility While Playing TRPGs, (3) Our Stories, Our Selves: Enactment and Embodiment of Possibility Through Fantasy Role-Play, (4) Slaying the Cisheteropatriarchy: Exerting Agency, Power, and Resistance Through TRPGs, and (5) “Dungeons & Dragons Kept Me Alive”: Personal Significance and Material Impacts of TRPGs. In Study 2, 10 self-identified LGBTQ2S+ couples (N = 20) who play TRPGs together completed semi-structured dyadic interviews and individual post-interview surveys. My analysis generated five overarching themes encapsulating the contributions of couples’ shared TRPG experiences to their romantic relationships: (1) We’ve Got Each Other’s Backs: Navigating Safety, Vulnerability, and Building Trust Through TRPGs, (2) I See You, You See Me: Deepening Emotional Intimacy, Understanding, and Connection Through TRPGs, (3) The “We” Factor: Enhancing and Expressing Couple Identity and Cohesion Through TRPGs, (4) Partner, Know Thyself: Exploration and Expansion of Individual Identity and Self-Concept Through TRPGs, and (5) We in the World: TRPGs as Tools for Shared Social and Community Engagement. In an integrative discussion, I synthesize the collective implications of my analyses within the current socio-political landscape and reflect on the significance of imaginative play, relational connection, and joy as acts of resistance in the lives of LGBTQ2S+ individuals and their communities.
