TY  - THES
TI  - The Creation of Queer Space Using the Medieval
AU  - Buckley, Landon David
AB  - The purpose of this thesis is twofold, the first aim is to communicate an argument for the use of public scholarship aimed at evangelical Christians, and the political right at large. The second aim of this thesis is to examine the ways in which two separate medievalist entities use an aesthetic of the medieval to expand or constrict what gender and sexuality can be and argue for a future that sees the evangelical church opening its views to who gets to be included. The goal of my introduction is to readjust how scholars approach conservative audiences. Members of conservative evangelicalism cannot be treated as a monolith of thought. To treat them as such, and to abandon them to the worst of what their school of thought can be is to give up an entire portion of the populace to never expand their ideas for inclusivity. In this cultural moment, it is more important than ever that scholars adjust their work to broaden their reach. With the rise of anti-intellectualism, scholars need to adapt their messaging to push through the noise and share their expertise where it is needed. The bulk of this thesis is aimed at the conservative evangelical audience I speak about in the introduction, using a narrative format that uses personal story to ground its research and is familiar to that audience. I look first at a Catholic boy’s camp in Alberta by the name Arcatheos and its sister camp Captivenia. These two camps use an aesthetic of the medieval to form young people into strict gender roles, claiming authority through a misuse of the medieval. I examine these abuses before moving to contrast this against Dungeons and Dragons (D&D), a tabletop role-playing game that has become an increasingly inclusive space for the queer community in the last decade. I use D&D as a precedent for a path forward for the Church into a more inclusive reality.
