TY  - JOUR
TI  - Learning to trust immigrants: Testing a game-based experiential role-playing intervention in the United States
AU  - Bouchillon, Brandon C.
T2  - Computers in Human Behavior Reports
AB  - This study examined whether a computer-based role-playing game in which participants created immigrant characters from Mexico and helped them naturalize to the United States could foster trust in the average person, trust in legal immigrants, and trust in Mexican immigrants specifically. A pretest-posttest experiment with two role-playing conditions and a control condition was conducted (N = 750). One of the role-playing conditions included a pre-recorded video from an actress portraying a Mexican immigrant, while the other did not. The video was used as a form of indirect contact. Sample demographics were matched to U.S. Census percentages. Role-playing with indirect contact led to greater trust in the average person compared to the control condition. Role-playing with and without indirect contact both led to significantly greater trust in legal immigrants compared to control. Finally, baseline trust in Mexican immigrants moderated the effect of role-playing with indirect contact on Mexican immigrant trust in the posttest. The largest gains were found among participants who were the least trusting initially. Results suggest that role-playing games featuring indirect contact with the outgroup could promote trust among those who would otherwise tend to withhold it. This addresses the tendency for the contact hypothesis in its traditional form to preach to the choir.
