TY  - CHAP
TI  - From Opposition to Transformation: Building Resilience with Pretensive Methodologies
AU  - Hartyándi, Mátyás
T2  - Designing Immersive Participatory Practices for Enacting Eco-Social Transformations
A2  - Light, Ann
A2  - Harry Lewis
AB  - This chapter explores how playful and dramatic approaches to learning and development, in other words, pretensive methodologies, can foster resilience. Pretensive methodologies are rooted in imagination, spontaneity and joint pretence, our capacity to inhabit fictional scenarios as if they were real. By encouraging creative thinking, decisive action and innovative problem-solving, pretensive methodologies bridge theory and practice and are capable of building resilience in individuals and communities facing uncertainty or crisis. The study highlights their transformative power, demonstrating how they help people overcome challenges, adapt to change and grow through play and exploration. Following a brief personal context, the chapter clarifies the multifaceted concept of resilience and the constraints that shape social transformation. It then demystifies the terminology of pretensive practices and presents a comparative framework for understanding their differences. The chapter concludes with an analysis of key methodological families, namely applied gaming (gaming simulations, analogue serious games, tabletop role-playing games), applied drama (process drama, Theatre of the Oppressed, larp, Chinese jubensha) and further practices (psychodrama, sociodrama, constellation work, bodywork). Together, these approaches show how games, simulations, role-play and theatre can build personal and collective resilience by enabling people to rehearse change and co-create immersive alternative futures.
