TY  - CONF
TI  - WIP: Leveling Up Learning: A Dungeons & Dragons-Inspired Approach to Teaching Object-Oriented Programming
AU  - Thomas, Cassandra
AU  - Ashby, Iryna
AU  - Exter, Marisa
T2  - 2025 IEEE Frontiers in Education Conference (FIE)
AB  - This WIP research-to-practice paper explores a novel pedagogical strategy for teaching object-oriented programming (OOP) to Generation Z learners using an approach inspired by Dungeons & Dragons. Recognizing that traditional instructional methods often fail to engage today's students—who prefer interactive, visual, and personalized learning experiences this project integrates narrative role-play and gamification into a CS2 course assignment. Designed collaboratively by instructional designers and computer science faculty from two partner institutions, including a Minority-Serving Institution, the initiative aligns with broader efforts to infuse culturally responsive, interdisciplinary pedagogy into computing education. The redesigned assignment required students to apply core OOP concepts—such as inheritance, encapsulation, and polymorphism—through creating fantasy characters, designing class hierarchies, and simulating combat scenarios. Students developed C++ classes using UML diagrams and object-oriented design principles, with additional layers of gameplay involving randomized dice rolls and strategic decision-making. Students could choose from various programming and non-programming challenges, many of which were optional and offered opportunities for creative expression and differentiated skill application. Findings from two semesters of implementation suggest that integrating narrative elements and optional challenge tiers boosted student motivation and supported learning preferences typical of Generation Z, such as autonomy, interactivity, and frequent, ongoing feedback. However, the second semester was marked by unexpected disruptions that resulted in the need for hybrid instruction. This created challenges for collaboration, as well as unequal participation and compressed timelines. This paper demonstrates the value of using immersive, gameinspired strategies to teach abstract programming concepts while fostering creativity, engagement, and teamwork. It offers practical implications for educators seeking to design active learning experiences that can meet the needs of a diverse student body in computing disciplines.
