TY  - JOUR
TI  - GLOSS
AU  - Benincasa, Marco
AB  - GLOSS (the Generic Lightweight One-Shot System) is a rules-light role-playing system primarily designed for quick use in one-shots. Mechanically, it draws inspiration from various schools of thought in the tabletop role-playing sphere, primarily the 2d6 (two six-sided dice) backbone of the D6-tradition, the idea of statements as building blocks for characters also found in narrative games like Risus and the Fate family of games or the principle of rolling under a set threshold used in many percentile-style games. The abstraction of health and distances into stages/tracks/range bands reflect the streamlined design philosophy found in many lightweight RPGs, while the variable damage takes inspiration from the TinyD6 set of games specifically. Furthermore, the Boost Points mechanic takes inspiration from action economy and support systems found e.g. in Savage Worlds and, more recently, also Shadowrun. All games mentioned are the property of their respective creators and are referenced here only as design influences.

GLOSS is furthermore intended to be usable to tell stories of any genre. Omitting detailed skill lists in favor of narrative statements as basic character building blocks allows for a ‘cunning hacker’ and a ‘noble knight’ to exist side by side without intense preparation by the Game Master (GM). Weapon categories and equipment guidelines are correspondingly broad defined for this purpose.

Though it is obvious that such a light ruleset puts additional responsibility in the GM’s hands, we are convinced, following the OSR-postulate “rulings over rules,” that this design choice fits the intended application of the system at hand. It is furthermore assumed that the reader (likely the future GM) possesses a basic knowledge of role-playing games, concepts and terminology. Thus, definitions of popular terms as well as the obligatory ‘What is a role-playing game’ section were omitted. In the same vein, examples are kept to a minimum.

Part 1 of this booklet introduces the core rules, recommended to be used in every game played in the GLOSS framework. Part 2 introduces additional rules and rule applications to adjust the ruleset to the kind of game the GM and players intend to play. For this purpose, a table listing core, recommended and optional rules for the most popular genres in tabletop role-playing is provided.
