TY  - THES
TI  - TTRPG for children with war traumas
AU  - Alqawasma, Raghd
AB  - This dissertation research examines how children who are in conflict areas and have been exposed to war trauma experience TTRPGs and how therapists and caregivers can utilize them in group therapy sessions. The use of play therapy has shown great success, but there are many limitations to accessibility, especially in remote areas, war zones, or areas of displacement. This research draws upon the theories of play therapy, narrative therapy, and trauma-informed design to create a TTRPG that can be used in structured, symbolic, and cooperative play, where children can express their emotions and regain a sense of control over their lives, while it may help therapists in the screening phase to determine which child needs one-to-one therapy. The development of this TTRPG and research is achieved through a review of various therapeutic and emotional board games, interviews with psychologists and therapists working with war-exposed children, and prototypes of the tabletop role-playing game. The tabletop role-playing game is specifically developed to be used in low-resource and low-literacy environments. The game utilizes symbolic storytelling and cooperative play to allow for maximum flexibility and to create an environment where all participants feel safe and comfortable in expressing themselves emotionally without fear of re-traumatization. Ultimately, the result of this research will be a playable TTRPG prototype, which can be utilized in both group and individual therapy sessions and can serve as an observation tool for professionals to identify children who may need additional psychological assistance.
