TY  - THES
TI  - Investigating the Feasibility and Acceptability of TTRPGs as a Therapeutic Modality for the Treatment of Socially Anxious Adolescents
AU  - Vitucci, Daniel J.
AB  - VITUCCI, DANIEL J, Ph.D., August 2026, Clinical Psychology Investigating the Feasibility and Acceptability of TTRPGs as a Therapeutic Modality for the Treatment of Socially Anxious Adolescents Director of Dissertation: Julie S. Owens Social anxiety disorder (SAD) is a common and impairing adolescent mental health disorder, yet many youth do not receive or remain engaged in evidence-based treatment. This dissertation evaluated the feasibility, acceptability, safety, and preliminary clinical utility of a therapist-guided tabletop role-playing game (TTRPG) intervention integrating cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT) principles. Study 1 used expert review and pilot implementation with five adults to refine the intervention. Study 2 piloted the revised eight-session intervention with 10 adolescents exhibiting elevated social anxiety. Outcomes included feasibility, engagement, satisfaction, safety, Subjective Units of Distress (SUDs), and changes in social anxiety symptoms. Attendance and engagement were high, and adolescents and caregivers reported strong acceptability and satisfaction. No adverse events or evidence of iatrogenic effects were observed. Exposure activities consistently elicited clinically meaningful distress, supporting the intervention's proposed mechanism of action. Although the study was not powered to detect statistically significant symptom change, several medium-to-large effect sizes suggested potential clinical benefit. These findings support the feasibility, acceptability, and safety of therapist-guided TTRPGs and warrant future controlled trials evaluating their efficacy for adolescent social anxiety.
