AU  - Samones, E.
AU  - Phan, T.
AU  - Smith, D.
AU  - Savino, P.B.
T2  - Annals of Emergency Medicine
AB  - Methods: The CER course focused on resuscitation, decontamination, and triage principles for interprofessional teams via both traditional high-fidelity manikin simulations and virtual reality simulation. Mixed senior medicine, nursing, pharmacy, and allied health student team events included simulations of psychiatric emergencies, toxic exposures, hemorrhagic shock, and a virtual reality mass casualty triage event. Impact was measured using a mandatory pre/post self-assessment on a five-point Likert scale. Changes in self-assessed confidence percentages from 2022–2024 were tracked, with descriptive analysis performed.
Results: Across the three years analyzed (2022–2024), participants consistently demonstrated meaningful increases in self-rated confidence and role responsibility following CER training. The combined proportion of respondents selecting the top Likert categories (4 and 5) increased by 10 to 31 percentage points per question. The largest gains were seen in students’ self-rated confidence, with absolute increases of 29.4% (2024), 31.5% (2023), and 26.9% (2022). Participants also reported that establishing clear roles improved communication, organization, and confidence during simulated crises.
Conclusion: Self-assessed ratings of both role responsibility and confidence in responding to a critical event improved following CER training in all years analyzed. By placing medical, nursing, pharmacy, and allied health students into shared highacuity simulations, the CER course allowed learners to practice communication, role delineation, and coordinated decision-making in a realistic team-based environment. Students previously reported feeling underprepared for critical events, citing limited exposure to triage and mass casualty training in their formal education. Our findings suggest that adapting prehospital frameworks such as emphasizing role responsibility
